2186187987 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187987 1084 #uuid "cf7c6882-5ee0-582e-9c20-d88f20cbc8bb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187988 1081 "1" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187988 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187988 1084 #uuid "f3577af9-f328-5719-878e-00ab5db29b64" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187989 1081 "2" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187989 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187989 1084 #uuid "fe409fc8-4c4a-54fa-b0d2-770d4a223ef0" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187990 1081 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187990 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187990 1084 #uuid "1df6e81f-e664-599e-8b46-e3b74702184b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187991 1081 "0" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187991 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187991 1084 #uuid "662d4eb9-6e0d-5bfc-82b2-5a61a49e771c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1069 "e-10033" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1071 "![](images/bd/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta$, shown above." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1072 #uuid "deaef5cc-99fb-57e5-a6f2-22f17a7f00af" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187992 1074 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nr=-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2\\cos θ\\cos θ =-2\\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= -2\\cos θ\\sin θ =-\\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$.\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (-2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; =-2 \\cdot 2\\cos θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = 4\\sin θ\\cos θ; = 2\\sin 2θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (-\\sin 2θ)^{′}; = - (\\cos 2θ \\cdot 2); =-2\\cos 2θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2\\cos 2θ}{2\\sin 2θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187993 1069 "e-10037" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187993 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $50$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.5$, find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187993 1072 #uuid "2f27b473-e10a-5e77-8bb2-d8ae5d0ecf4a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187993 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=50, \\qquad n = 9, \\qquad s = 1.5\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(T > 3.355) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{8,0.005} = 3.355\n$$\n\n![](images/84/84f060ec1a5e8a8b3db0c0866ebef9db1c242e944717a5abfd28447e0eddb9be.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}50 & ± 3.355 \\cdot \\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ 50 & ± 1.678\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187994 1069 "e-10040" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187994 1071 "A tire manufacturer measures the stopping distance of a new tire model from $60 \\, \\textrm{mph}$ on a standard test car. A sample of $81$ stopping distances yielded a sample mean of $132\\, \\textrm{ft}$ with a sample standard deviation of $5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}$. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the mean stopping distance $\\mu$ of these tires.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $79$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.640\\,$ |\n| $80$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.374\\,$ | $\\,2.639\\,$ |\n| $81$ | $\\,1.292\\,$ | $\\,1.664\\,$ | $\\,1.990\\,$ | $\\,2.373\\,$ | $\\,2.638\\,$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187994 1072 #uuid "c2e2bcbc-ab4b-5a0a-a803-cb98326017ec" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187994 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 81 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=132 \\, \\textrm{ft}, \\qquad n = 81, \\qquad s = 5.4 \\, \\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value $t_{80,0.025}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{80, 0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/d5/d513fbeee39a42a5e897c1b171d7e820196066517c5aa1c6c12549a98ce00ddd.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nt_{80,0.025}=1.990{:}\n$$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}80}$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.990}$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}132 & ± 1.99 \\cdot \\frac{5.4}{\\sqrt{81}}ft \\\\ 132 & ± 1.194ft\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187995 1069 "e-10047" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187995 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $30$, and the sample standard deviation is $8$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.66) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $99$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187995 1072 #uuid "7446f45b-6d41-5a11-8006-853b38fa5142" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187995 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance $\\sigma^2$. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 100 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad n = 100, \\qquad s = 8\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(T > 1.66) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_{n-1,\\alpha/2} = t_{99,0.05} = 1.66\n$$\n\n![](images/8d/8d4cd3a76fffee915059c849d3bac4ca395deb3e2cfe5ab1701e9ee4737b4401.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}30 & ± 1.66 \\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 30 & ± 1.328\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187996 1069 "e-10056" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187996 1071 "For what values of $p$ will both $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{2p}{3}\\right)^{n}$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2n}\\right)^{p}$ converge?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187996 1072 #uuid "c2237a43-c7a0-5771-ab2a-10ff11d3b53f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187996 1074 "Let's examine each of our series in turn:\n\n- The first series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{2p}{3}\\right)^{n}$\nis a geometric series with common ratio $r=\\dfrac{2p}{3}$. For convergence, we require $\\mid r \\mid \\lt 1$. So, this first series converges for\n$\\mid \\frac{2p}{3} \\mid \\mid < 1; \\frac{2}{3} \\mid p \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid p \\mid \\mid < \\frac{3}{2}; - \\frac{3}{2} \\mid < p < \\frac{3}{2}$.\n- The second series\n$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2n}\\right)^{p} = \\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^p \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{n^p}$\nis a $p$-series, and converges for $p \\gt 1$.\n\nFor both series to converge, we need both of the following inequalities to be satisfied:\n\n$$\n{-\\frac{3}{2} < p < \\frac{3}{2}; p > 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, both series converge provided that\n\n$$\n1 \\lt p \\lt \\dfrac 3 2\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187997 1069 "e-10057" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187997 1071 "For what values of $p$ will both $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^{p+1}}$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (p+1)^n$ converge?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187997 1072 #uuid "6822588b-611f-5178-8b6a-8dc9435c8efc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187997 1074 "Let's examine each of our series in turn:\n\n- The first series\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^{p+1}}$\nis an alternating $p$-series and converges for\n$\\begin{bmatrix}p + 1 & > 0 \\\\ p & > - 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- The second series\n$\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} (p+1)^n$\nis a geometric series with common ratio $r = p+1$. For convergence, we require $\\mid r \\mid < 1$. So, this series converges for\n$\\mid p + 1 \\mid \\mid < 1; - 1 < p + 1\\begin{vmatrix}< 1 \\\\ -2 < p\\end{vmatrix}< 0$.\n\nFor both series to converge, we need both of the following inequalities to be satisfied:\n${p > - 1; - 2 < p < 0$\n\nTherefore, both series converge provided that $-1 < p < 0$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187998 1069 "e-10058" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187998 1071 "For which values of $p$ is the following sum convergent?\n\n$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{2p+1}}+\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{n^{8-p}}$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187998 1072 #uuid "73ac0434-ea20-53b5-9bb4-7768f6b2383d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187998 1074 "The sum is convergent if both series are individually convergent.\n\nFor the first series to be convergent, we require\n$2p+1 > 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in (0,\\infty)$.\n\nFor the second series to be convergent, we require\n\n$$\n8-p> 1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\in (-\\infty, 7)\n$$\n\nFinding the intersection of the two solutions gives\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty) \\cap (-\\infty, 7) = (0,7)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0 < p < 7$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187999 1069 "e-10060" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187999 1071 "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n$X_i \\sim N(2,3^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 6$,\ncalculate $P(10\\leq Y\\leq 14)$, where $Y = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{6}$.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6103 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6141 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6480 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6844 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6879 \\,$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187999 1072 #uuid "f8b96980-910c-5e33-b6bf-e1f8532dcdbc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186187999 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, \\: n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have\n\n$$\nY = X_{1} + X_{2} + ⋯ + X_{6} \\mid ∼N(6 \\cdot 2, 6 \\cdot 3^{2}); ∼N(6 \\cdot 2, 6 \\cdot 9); = N(12, 54)\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability\n\n$$\nP(10\\leq Y \\leq 14)\n$$\n\nWe first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(10 \\le Y \\le 14) &= P(\\frac{10 - 12}{\\sqrt{54}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{14 - 12}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\\n&= P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\\n&= P(-0.27 \\le Z \\le 0.27) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.27) - Φ(-0.27) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.27) - (1 - Φ(0.27)) \\\\\n&= 2Φ(0.27) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.27) = 0.6064\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\leq Y \\leq 14) = 2\\Phi(0.27) - 1 = 0.2128\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1069 "e-10072" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1071 "Since the year $2000$, a herd of antelopes multiplies at a rate proportional to their population. Also, the number of antelopes in the herd decreases by $20$ antelopes per year due to predators. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the population of antelopes at time $t$, where $t$ is the time (in years) since the year $2000$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1072 #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188000 1074 "The rate of change in the number of antelopes per year increases in proportion to $P$ and decreases by $20$. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t}=kP - 20\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1069 "e-10075" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate inversely proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1072 #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188001 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to its radius.* Therefore,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{k}{r}$,\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{4}{3}πr^{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= 4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dV}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n4πr^{2}\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{r} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= ((\\frac{k}{r}))/(4πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{k}{4πr^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{4\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{K}{r^3}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1069 "e-10076" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y'(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin. The derivative at the point $(-1,2)$ equals $2$. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1072 #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188002 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** $y'(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance from $P$ to the origin.***\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the distance from $P$ to the origin* is given by $x^2+y^2$.\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional to* the square of the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have\n$y'(x) = k\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}$\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\ny'(x) = \\dfrac{k}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $y'(x) = 2$ when at the point $(-1, 2)$. We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (k)/((-1)^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{k}{5} \\\\\nk &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\ny'(x)= \\dfrac{10}{x^2+y^2}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188003 1069 "e-10078" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188003 1071 "Given that $a_n = \\dfrac{6^n}{(n+4)!}$ for $n\\geq 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$\n2. $a_n$ is an increasing sequence\n3. $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188003 1072 #uuid "b8979f5e-53eb-52de-b8a9-3588100b32a6" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188003 1074 "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{6^n}{(n+4)!}\n$$\n\nis positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$\\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}|= (((6^{n + 1})/((n + 5)!)))/(((6^{n})/((n + 4)!))); = (6^{n + 1} \\cdot (n + 4)!)/(6^{n} \\cdot (n + 5)!); = (6^{n} \\cdot 6 \\cdot (n + 4)!)/(6^{n} \\cdot (n + 5) \\cdot (n + 4)!); = (6^{n} \\cdot 6 \\cdot (n + 4)!)/(6^{n} \\cdot (n + 5) \\cdot (n + 4)!); = \\frac{6}{n + 5}; \\le 1$.\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\lt L\\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188004 1069 "e-10079" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188004 1071 "Given that $a_n = \\dfrac{3}{2n^2}$ for $n\\geq 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$\n2. $a_n$ is an increasing sequence\n3. $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188004 1072 #uuid "18fb1e77-2504-5e05-b7f8-1a92b1f0320f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188004 1074 "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3}{2n^2}\n$$\n\nis positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$L|= \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}; = (((3)/(2(n + 1)^{2})))/((\\frac{3}{2n^{2}})); = (n^{2})/((n + 1)^{2}); = (\\frac{n}{n + 1})^{2}$.\nNow, since $n \\geq 1$, it follows that\n$\\dfrac{n}{n + 1} \\lt 1$,\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore\n$L = \\left(\\dfrac{n}{n + 1}\\right)^2 \\lt 1$.\nSince the sequence is positive for all $n$, we can conclude that\n$0 \\lt L \\lt 1$.\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\lt L \\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188005 1069 "e-10091" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188005 1071 "Let $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{110} \\sim U[1,5]$ be an I.I.D. sample of size $110$ from the continuous uniform distribution on the interval $[1,5]$. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = \\sum_{i=1}^{110} X_i$.\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188005 1072 #uuid "be43d5ab-221c-53c0-a3a4-a03c17d1af21" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188005 1074 "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nMoreover, if we define\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nthen, by the properties of mean and variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = n\\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = n\\sigma^2\n$$\n\nand consequently,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a uniform random variable $X_i \\sim U[a,b]$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 110, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{a + b}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{2} \\\\\n&= 3, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= ((b - a)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= ((5 - 1)^{2})/(12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of $X$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX \\mid ∼N(nμ, nσ^{2}); ∼N(110 \\cdot 3, 110 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}); ∼N(330, \\frac{440}{3})\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188006 1069 "e-10092" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188006 1071 "Let $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(0.4)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,80$ be independent geometric random variables. Suppose we define the random variable $X$ as\n$X = \\sum_{i=1}^{80} X_i$.\n\nWhat is the approximate distribution of $X$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188006 1072 #uuid "6cfeed33-4982-5c93-96b3-bd34e3603659" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188006 1074 "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nMoreover, if we define\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nthen by the properties of mean and variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = n\\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = n\\sigma^2\n$$\n\nand consequently,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a geometric random variable $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{p}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 80, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{0.4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - p}{p^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 0.4}{0.4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.6}{0.16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of $X$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX \\mid ∼N(nμ, nσ^{2}); ∼N(80 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}, 80 \\cdot \\frac{15}{4}); ∼N(200, 300)\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188007 1069 "e-10093" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188007 1071 "Given that $X_i\\sim B\\left(25,\\dfrac{9}{20}\\right)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,66$ are independent binomial random variables, what is the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188007 1072 #uuid "1205c813-39f9-517c-89ec-d237d901f09a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188007 1074 "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a binomial random variable $X_i \\sim B(m,p)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = mp, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = mp(1-p)\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 66, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= mp \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{45}{4}, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] \\\\\n&= mp(1 - p) \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\cdot \\frac{11}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{99}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(\\frac{45}{4}, \\frac{99}{16} \\cdot \\frac{1}{66}); ∼N(\\frac{45}{4}, \\frac{3}{32})\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188008 1069 "e-10097" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188008 1071 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n${\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_Y(y) = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$ for $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188008 1072 #uuid "175f3917-8801-515d-a493-5f36a52aa01b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188008 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y$, we get\n$f_{Y}(y)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dx; = \\frac{1}{4}e^{-y/2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$,\nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X$, we get\n$f_{X}(x)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{b}e^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-y/2}]_{0}^{b}; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-b/2} + 2]; = \\frac{x}{2}$,\nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$. So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$, we obtain that\n$\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188009 1069 "e-10102" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188009 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n${\\frac{4}{x^{3}y^{3}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, y \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind the expected value of $Y$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188009 1072 #uuid "f32f96bc-b02a-54fe-b370-8a384c6ae67e" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188009 1074 "The marginal density function for $Y$ when\n\n$$\ny \\geq 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(y) &= \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{4}{x^{3}y^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2x^{2}}]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}lim_(b → ∞)(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2b^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{y^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f_Y(y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{2}{y^{3}},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, the expected value of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y \\cdot f_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{∞}y \\cdot \\frac{2}{y^{3}}dy \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)\\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{y}]_{1}^{b} \\\\\n&= 2lim_(b → ∞)(1 - \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188010 1069 "e-10116" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188010 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $\\theta \\in \\left[0, 2\\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = 2\\cos2\\theta - 2\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta$,\nand $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188010 1072 #uuid "70e3ea0e-2f39-5715-98a3-2a0e686ffa76" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188010 1074 "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\nx'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta)$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{3π}{2})|= 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}); = 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (3π); = 0 + 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → 3π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → 3π/2)(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{2\\cos θ + 2\\sin 2θ}{2\\cos 2θ - 2\\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(((\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ)^{′})/((\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ)^{′})); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{-\\sin θ + 2\\cos 2θ}{-2\\sin 2θ - \\cos θ}); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}))/(-2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (3π))/(-2\\sin (3π) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (1 + 2 \\cdot (-1))/(-2 \\cdot 0 - 0); = \\frac{1 - 2}{0}; = \\frac{-1}{0}; \\ne 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0$, there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188011 1069 "e-10117" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188011 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0ory < 0 \\\\ x^{2}(1 - e^{-y}),\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, y \\ge 0; F_{Y}(y), \\mid x > 1, y \\ge 0$.\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\; y \\geq 0$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188011 1072 #uuid "4af79cd3-8c50-533f-a744-3784a71a15dc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188011 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial^2}{\\partial x \\partial y} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= \\frac{∂^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x, y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}(1 - e^{-y}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - e^{-y})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}e^{-y}) \\\\\n&= e^{-y} \\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= e^{-y} \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= 2xe^{-y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{2xe^{-y},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, y \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188012 1069 "e-10143" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188012 1071 "What is $0.05\\,\\textrm{km}$ expressed in $\\textrm{mm}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188012 1072 #uuid "0273bace-3b81-527a-a88e-b4bbf8f6fd30" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188012 1074 "There are $1000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, and also $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$. We can write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1000m}{km} &= \\frac{1000m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{1000mm}{m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{1000mm}{m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000 \\cdot 1000mm}{km} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000000mm}{km}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor. It tells us that there are $1\\,000\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{km}$.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.05 \\,\\textrm{km}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.05km &= 0.05km \\cdot \\frac{1000000mm}{km} \\\\\n&= 0.05km \\cdot \\frac{1000000mm}{km} \\\\\n&= 0.05 \\cdot 1000000mm \\\\\n&= 50000mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.05 \\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $50 \\,000 \\,\\textrm{mm}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188013 1069 "e-10149" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188013 1071 "Suppose we want to measure the volume of a shoe box. What units could we use?\n\n1. $\\textrm{cm}^2$\n2. $\\textrm{cm}^3$\n3. $\\textrm{kg}$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188013 1072 #uuid "6cbff12b-cc20-5db4-9e64-8bb7b4c62dd9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188013 1074 "We can measure length in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$. When measuring lengths in centimeters, volumes are measured in $\\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm} \\times \\textrm{cm}$, which is usually written as $\\textrm{cm}^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188014 1069 "e-10150" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188014 1071 "![](images/7e/7e21a9afeb5a6c102a8426255b17112479144845be87f121435c8e27078610ab.png)\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube shown in the diagram above?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188014 1072 #uuid "94639d5a-c922-52f3-8e50-82d0e9a3dbad" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188014 1074 "To find the volume $V$ of a cube, we cube its side length. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= (5m)^{3} \\\\\n&= 5m \\cdot 5m \\cdot 5m \\\\\n&= (5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (m \\cdot m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 125m^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188015 1069 "e-10163" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188015 1071 "![](images/7e/7e300a90dbfda5a9210cec502c2879b7950e2765c433eb6ee6284e74db3cd220.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the square above?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188015 1072 #uuid "5f153b4a-2706-5ff6-b0b3-65cfc29e1391" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188015 1074 "To calculate the area $A$ of a square, we square its side length. Therefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= (5ft)^{2} \\\\\n&= 5ft \\cdot 5ft \\\\\n&= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (ft \\cdot ft) \\\\\n&= 25ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188016 1069 "e-10165" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188016 1071 "What is $55\\,000 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ expressed in $\\textrm{kg}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188016 1072 #uuid "74ecc422-aa5a-5041-b13b-c379ece27882" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188016 1074 "There are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$, and also $1000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ per $\\textrm{g}$. We can write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1000g}{kg} &= \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\cdot \\frac{1000mg}{g} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\cdot \\frac{1000mg}{g} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000 \\cdot 1000mg}{kg} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000000mg}{kg}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that there are $1\\,000\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ per $\\textrm{kg}$. However, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{kg})$, we need to multiply $55\\,000 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{1\\,000\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $55\\,000 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n55000mg &= 55000mg \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000000mg} \\\\\n&= 55000mg \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000000mg} \\\\\n&= 55000 \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000000} \\\\\n&= \\frac{55000}{1000000}kg \\\\\n&= 0.055kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55\\,000 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.055 \\, \\textrm{kg}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188017 1069 "e-10166" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188017 1071 "How many hours are in $5$ weeks?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188017 1072 #uuid "b5db013d-2995-5a68-b5bc-3ee7e976e6c7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188017 1074 "There are $7\\,\\textrm {days}$ per $\\textrm{week}$, and also $24\\,\\textrm{hours}$ per $\\textrm{day}$. We can write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7day}{week} &= \\frac{7day}{week} \\cdot \\frac{24hour}{day} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7day}{week} \\cdot \\frac{24hour}{day} \\\\\n&= \\frac{168hour}{week}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $5\\,\\textrm{weeks}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5week &= 5week \\cdot \\frac{168hour}{week} \\\\\n&= 5week \\cdot \\frac{168hour}{week} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 168hour \\\\\n&= 840hour\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5$ weeks is equivalent to $840 \\,\\textrm{hours}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188018 1069 "e-10167" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188018 1071 "How many hours are in $18$ seconds?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188018 1072 #uuid "be93f7fe-6876-5ed7-aed6-4416b18b4b23" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188018 1074 "There are $60\\,\\textrm {min}$ per $\\textrm{hour}$, and also $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{60min}{hour} &= \\frac{60min}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60min}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3600s}{hour}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that there are $3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{hour}$. However, since we're converting from a smaller unit of time $(\\textrm{s})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{hours})$, we need to multiply $18 \\,\\textrm{s}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{hour}}{3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $18 \\,\\textrm{s}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18s &= 18s \\cdot \\frac{hour}{3600s} \\\\\n&= 18s \\cdot \\frac{hour}{3600s} \\\\\n&= 18 \\cdot \\frac{hour}{3600} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18}{3600}hour \\\\\n&= 0.005hour\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18$ seconds is equivalent to $0.005 \\, \\textrm{hours.}$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188019 1069 "e-10177" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188019 1071 "Which digits will make the following statement true when inserted into the empty box?\n\n$0$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188019 1072 #uuid "0c49ce22-2492-5778-89fd-55f9070bc815" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188019 1074 "Remember that \"$>$\" means \"greater than.\"\n\nLet's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $\\color{blue}2$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $0$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe then compare place values, *going from left to right*:\n\n- The digit $3$ in the *ten-thousands* place is the same for both numbers.\n- The digit $4$ in the *thousands* place is the same for both numbers.\n- Moving on to the *hundreds* place, we see that the first number is larger than the second when $0$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true when $0$ or $1$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188020 1069 "e-10178" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188020 1071 "Which digits will make the following statement true when inserted into the empty box?\n\n$0$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188020 1072 #uuid "b2ada053-33a0-57d5-b649-7099b7c55c3c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188020 1074 "Remember that \"$<$\" means \"less than.\"\n\nLet's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\color{red} 4$ | $5$ | $1$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $9$ | $2$ |\n\nWe then compare place values, *going from left to right*:\n\n- The digit $3$ in the *hundred-thousands* place is the same for both numbers.\n- Moving on to the *ten-thousands* place, we see that the first number is smaller than the second when $0$\n\nTherefore, the statement is true when $0$ or $9$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188021 1069 "e-10179" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188021 1071 "![](images/26/26a36f1e5a13f661e1226bb22bc00ce1b991640f5e0c89c8e54c8b7904daa3b8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a number line. Which of the following numbers could be placed into the empty box?\n\n1. $485$\n2. $6,567$\n3. $10,001$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188021 1072 #uuid "61f5f462-b4d4-5640-bd73-c2fdc216e296" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188021 1074 "Since the missing number lies to the *left* of the number $5,285$, it must be *less than* this number. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0000\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $485$ satisfies the condition since it has fewer digits than $5,285$.\n$3$\n- The number $6,567$ does *not* satisfies the condition. Let's compare place values, *going from left to right*:In the *hundreds* place, we see that $6 > 5$. **thousands****hundreds****tens****ones**$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5}$$2$$8$$5$$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6}$$5$$6$$7$So, $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{6},567 >\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{5},285$.\n- The number $10,001$ does *not* satisfy the condition since it has more digits than $5,285$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188022 1069 "e-10199" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188022 1071 "Use the box model below to find the **quotient** of $72 \\div 5$.\n\n|  | $10$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $7$$2$$-$$0$$0$$0$$0$ | $0$$0$$-$$0$$0$$\\fbox{2}$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188022 1072 #uuid "6bddbfe5-c651-5dfd-8d0d-9286dd30eea2" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188022 1074 "First, we subtract\n\n$$\n5 \\times {\\color{blue}10} = 50\n$$\n\nfrom $72$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $7$$2$$-$$5$$0$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}2$ |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}22$ to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $7$$2$$-$$5$$0$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}2$ |\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n5 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 20\n$$\n\nis less than $22$, we subtract $5 \\times {\\color{blue}4}$ from $22$.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $7$$2$$-$$5$$0$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}2$$\\color{red}2$$-$$2$$0$$\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nWe can't subtract any further since $\\fbox{2}$ is less than $5$. Hence, $2$ is the remainder.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10 + 4 = 14\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188023 1069 "e-10200" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188023 1071 "Suzy baked $97$ cookies for a charity sale and distributed them into packages, each containing $8$ cookies. Use the box model below to find how many packages she prepared and how many cookies were left unpackaged.\n\n|  | $10$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $9$$7$ |  |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188023 1072 #uuid "8cc8ccc2-a515-515e-acbd-f09c8f4f170f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188023 1074 "To find out how many many packages of cookies Suzy prepared and how many cookies were left unpackaged, we have to divide $97$ by $8$.\n\nFirst, we subtract $8 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $97$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $9$$7$$-$$8$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$ |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}17$ to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $9$$7$$-$$8$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$ |\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n8 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 16\n$$\n\nis less than $17$, we subtract $8 \\times {\\color{blue}2}$ from $17$.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $9$$7$$-$$8$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}7$$-$$1$$6$$\\fbox{1}$ |\n\nWe can't subtract any further since $\\fbox{1}$ is less than $8$. Hence, $1$ is the remainder.\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+2=12\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n97 \\div 8 = 12 \\,\\text{R}\\, 1\n$$\n\nSo, Suzy prepared $12$ packages of cookies, and $1$ cookie was left unpackaged." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188024 1069 "e-10208" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188024 1071 "$\\dfrac{2}{3} \\,\\square \\,\\dfrac{4}{9}$\n\nUsing a benchmark of $\\dfrac12$, determine which of the following symbols could replace the empty box above to make the statement true.\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188024 1072 #uuid "da54b645-86bf-50e8-9c8f-ff7c323eb6fc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188024 1074 "Let's compare each fraction to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac {\\color{red}{2}} {\\color{blue}{3}}$ is *greater than* $\\dfrac 1 2$ because ${\\color{red}{2}}$ is *greater than* ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\div 2 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 1\\,\\dfrac12$.\n\n![](images/6b/6b81b2620fd576b22cb05e6f21d309e649ba0f8086a69309f15dc17407a38576.png)\n\n- The fraction $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}} {\\color{blue}{9}}$ is *less than* $\\dfrac 1 2$ because ${\\color{red}{4}}$ is *less than* ${\\color{blue}{9}}\\div 2 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\n![](images/cb/cbe918cf5678d022bd53cebf467a56f45ad81d03cc340b503bd5c38d95c8e289.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nis *greater than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nbut\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nis *less than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nthis means that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nis *greater than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} > \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/f6/f60d658b9c07e4f6815e987b756ec32b619e966521c345e4df4d77706b67bcdf.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188025 1069 "e-10209" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188025 1071 "$\\dfrac 74 \\,\\square \\,\\dfrac 8 5$\n\nBy converting both fractions to mixed numbers, determine which symbols could replace the empty box above to make the statement true.\n\n1. $=$\n2. $>$\n3. $<$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188025 1072 #uuid "57e04a9e-c52f-51cf-8755-4c2b24f8b3f5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188025 1074 "First, let's convert our improper fractions to mixed numbers:\n\n- Converting $\\dfrac74$ to a mixed number, we get\n$\\frac{7}{4}|= 7 \\div 4; = 1R3; = 1\\frac{3}{4}$.\n- Converting $\\dfrac85$ to a mixed number, we get\n$\\frac{8}{5}|= 8 \\div 5; = 1R3; = 1\\frac{3}{5}$.\n\nSo, the problem statement is now as follows:\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac34 \\,\\,\\square \\,\\,1\\dfrac35\n$$\n\nSince the whole number part is the same for both fractions, we only need to compare the fractional parts.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac35\n$$\n\n(the numerators are the same, and quarters are larger than fifths), we have that\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac34 \\,\\, > \\,\\,1\\dfrac35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac74 > \\dfrac85\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188026 1069 "e-10230" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188026 1071 "Complete the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ The number $903,784$ has $9$ $\\underline{\\phantom{0000000000000}}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188026 1072 #uuid "f7ef44d4-4fde-54e5-99e1-00ca73c92d62" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188026 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}9$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n\nWe have $9$ in the hundred-thousands place. So, the complete statement is as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The number $903,784$ has $9$ hundred-thousands\".*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188027 1069 "e-10231" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188027 1071 "Complete the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ The number $3,275,164$ has $3$ $\\underline{\\phantom{0000000000000}}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188027 1072 #uuid "52d138c5-bbaf-5409-a7d7-769045e77605" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188027 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}3$ | $2$ | $7$ | $5$ | $1$ | $6$ | $4$ |\n\nWe have $3$ in the millions place. So, the complete statement is as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The number $3,275,164$ has $3$ millions\".*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188028 1069 "e-10232" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188028 1071 "What number has $5$ millions and $5$ hundreds?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188028 1072 #uuid "a6e62ca5-e1ef-5b9d-a11d-8f0f4fa6930f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188028 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\nWe have $5$ millions and $5$ hundreds. For the other place values, we just write zero.\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $5$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nSo our number is $5,000,500$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188029 1069 "e-10244" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188029 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta)$,\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{+}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{-}; \\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{-}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{+}$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188029 1072 #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188029 1074 "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta)$, we get\n$x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})|=-\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})\\csc (\\frac{π}{2})); = 0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1); = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+$, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cot θ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cot θ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cotθ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cotθ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ})$.\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\csc θ + \\sin θ)|= \\csc (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}); = 1 + 1; = 2$.\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{-}}; = - ∞$.\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{+}}; = ∞$.\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188030 1069 "e-10246" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188030 1071 "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188030 1072 #uuid "d97c3cf2-b627-5274-853a-be3d7e5cda40" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188030 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2)$, and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,2$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcd6d6800caf6abdca868bba4abff3935f26e984807f13c25d4368dc858f2c1.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2) &= 4 \\\\\nf(-2) &= f(2) + \\int_{2}^{-2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(2) - \\int_{-2}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 \\\\\n&=-4 \\\\\nf(-6) &= f(2) + \\int_{2}^{-6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(2) - \\int_{-6}^{2}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 4 - (-6 + 8) \\\\\n&= 4 - 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{4,-4,2\\} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}}=4\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max}=2\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188031 1069 "e-10247" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188031 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9, 3$ and $5$, respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6]$ given that $f(6) = 2$.\n\n![](images/ab/ab98f2f27ff11ddb40138bf09900a865ca006bc8f26c95dfe03ea17fd4e49387.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188031 1072 #uuid "b28b6939-847d-51ab-a97d-4367323d0d01" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188031 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-6,6)$, and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3$. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,0,3,6$.\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(6) &= 2 \\\\\nf(3) &= f(6) + \\int_{6}^{3}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(6) - \\int_{3}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 2 - (-5) \\\\\n&= 7 \\\\\nf(0) &= f(6) + \\int_{6}^{0}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(6) - \\int_{0}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 2 - (3 - 5) \\\\\n&= 4 \\\\\nf(-6) &= f(6) + \\int_{6}^{-6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(6) - \\int_{-6}^{6}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= 2 - (-9 + 3 - 5) \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{2, 7, 4, 13\\} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_\\text{min} = 2\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{min} = 6\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188032 1069 "e-10248" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188032 1071 "![](images/69/69d2d66022521424f9c6cda6ad901eb9ed1dbdb01044f5fa2d2954049770a3d4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,4]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9$, $7$ and $8$, respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]$ given that $f(1) = 10$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188032 1072 #uuid "71cc1f65-437f-5928-82c2-be3dc4b0985f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188032 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4)$, and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=1$. Therefore, the candidates are\n\n$$\nx=-4,-2,1,4\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 10 \\\\\nf(-2) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{-2}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= f(1) - \\int_{-2}^{1}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 10 - (-7) \\\\\n&= 10 + 7 \\\\\n&= 17 \\\\\nf(-4) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{-4}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= f(1) - \\int_{-4}^{1}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 10 - (-9 - 7) \\\\\n&= 10 - (-16) \\\\\n&= 10 + 16 \\\\\n&= 26 \\\\\nf(4) &= f(1) + \\int_{1}^{4}f^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= 10 + 8 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{10,17,26,18\\} = 26\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_\\text{max} = 26\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{max} = -4\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188033 1069 "e-10249" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188033 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,5]$. The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2$, $14$ and $2$, respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ given that $f(3) = -2$.\n\n![](images/de/deb5d80fe70d31fe768a180f3afd8a6054eda8e9fc3d1d0cd52c696c9de47660.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188033 1072 #uuid "708db737-4215-5df0-8940-c9d17dbd2b5b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188033 1074 "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-5,5)$, and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3$. Therefore, the candidates are\n\n$$\nx=-5,-3,3,5\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(3) &= -2 \\\\\nf(5) &= f(3) + \\int_{3}^{5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 + (-2) \\\\\n&=-4 \\\\\nf(-3) &= f(3) + \\int_{3}^{-3}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(3) - \\int_{-3}^{3}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 - 14 \\\\\n&=-16 \\\\\nf(-5) &= f(3) + \\int_{3}^{-5}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&= f(3) - \\int_{-5}^{3}f^{′}(x)dx \\\\\n&=-2 - (-2 + 14) \\\\\n&=-14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2$, we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{-2, -4, -16, -14\\} = -16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is\n\n$$\nf_\\text{min} = -16\n$$\n\nand the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{min} = -3\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188034 1069 "e-10251" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188034 1071 "Write the number $607$ in words." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188034 1072 #uuid "4cbbea40-f6bb-587e-a37a-b5580aac7d38" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188034 1074 "To write a three-digit number in words, we\n\n- write the number of hundreds, followed by \"hundred,\"\n- then, write the tens and ones part.\n\nLet's write down the place values of $607$:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}6$ | $\\color{red}0$ | $\\color{red}7$ |\n\nNow, we write the number in words:\n\n- The number has $\\color{blue}6$ hundreds, so we write \"six\" followed by \"hundred\":$\\qquad$ \"*6 hundred*\"\n- The number has $\\color{red}0$ tens and $\\color{red}7$ ones, so we add \"seven\": $\\qquad$ \"*six hundred seven*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $607$ written in words is *six hundred seven*." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188035 1069 "e-10259" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188035 1071 "Write the number $32,042$ in words." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188035 1072 #uuid "ff6cb031-6451-5c3d-bc54-f4485aabbd89" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188035 1074 "Notice that the highest-value digit is in the ten-thousands place.\n\nLet's start by splitting the number into its periods:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{32},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{042}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at each period:\n\n- $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{32}$ has $3$ tens and $2$ ones. So, we write \"thirty-two\" followed by \"thousand,\":$\\qquad$ \"*thirty-two thousand,*\"\n- $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{042}$ has $0$ hundreds, $4$ tens, and $2$ ones. So, we add \"forty-two\":$\\qquad$ \"*thirty-two thousand, forty-two*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $32,042$ written in words is *thirty-two thousand, forty-two*." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188036 1069 "e-10262" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188036 1071 "Write the number $15,081,012$ in words." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188036 1072 #uuid "7366792c-08ce-554b-afde-cc160d3cc975" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188036 1074 "Notice that the highest-value digit is in the ten-millions place.\n\nLet's start by splitting the number into its periods:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{15},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{081},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{012}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at each period:\n\n- $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{15}$ has $1$ ten and $5$ ones. So, we write \"fifteen,\" followed by \"million,\":$\\qquad$ \"*fifteen million,*\"\n- $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{081}$ has $8$ tens and $1$ one. So, we add \"eighty-one,\" followed by \"thousand,\":$\\qquad$ \"*fifteen million, eighty-one thousand,*\"\n- $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{012}$ has $1$ ten and $2$ ones. So, we add \"twelve\":$\\qquad$ \"*fifteen million, eighty-one thousand, twelve*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $15,081,012$ written in words is *fifteen million, eighty-one thousand, twelve*." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188037 1069 "e-10280" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188037 1071 "Consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$5 \\times 4 = 20$\n\nFrom left to right, what numbers could be inserted into the following to make it a true statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ \"*The number $\\textrm{___}$ is $5$ times larger than $\\textrm{___}$.*\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188037 1072 #uuid "2e75b5d5-bcb2-5f3b-8ce4-4825afbd8e3d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188037 1074 "The given equation compares the size of the product $(20)$ to its factors $(4$ and $5)$. It can be interpreted as a comparison in the following two ways:\n\n- \"*The number $20$ is $5$ times larger than $4$.*\"\n- \"*The number $20$ is $4$ times larger than $5$.*\"\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $20$ and $4$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188038 1069 "e-10281" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188038 1071 "Which of the following equations shows that $56$ is $8$ times larger than $7$?\n\n1. $8 \\times 7= 56$\n2. $8 + 7 = 56$\n3. $7 \\times 56 = 8$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188038 1072 #uuid "c6e3038c-cf42-5899-bc9d-eb38ea587390" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188038 1074 "Let's examine our statement, highlighting some important words:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Which of the following equations shows that $56$ is $8$ **times larger** than $7$?*\n\nSo, the statement compares the size of a *product* $(56)$ to its *factors* $(8$ and $7)$. It can be interpreted as a comparison in the following two ways:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 7 =56, \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 7 \\times 8 = 56\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n8\\times 7 = 56\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188039 1069 "e-10282" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188039 1071 "Four employees are asked to hand out flyers in a shopping mall to promote a pizzeria. If they each received $40$ flyers, what equation could we use to find how many flyers they were given in total? Use $F$ to represent the total number of flyers." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188039 1072 #uuid "1e32fed2-1c9c-5019-acc8-635d775692a7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188039 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important parts of the given information:\n\n- Each employee received $40$ flyers.\n- The task of handing out the flyers was given to $4$ employees.\n\nSo, the total number of flyers, $F$, is $4$ **times larger** than the number of flyers given to each employee, $40$. This can be represented by the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\n40 \\times 4 = F \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 4 \\times 40 = F\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188040 1069 "e-10302" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188040 1071 "| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 3)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=3\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 3)$ is shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $\\mu_{Y \\mid 3} = \\dfrac{8}{3}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188040 1072 #uuid "6cdc2b0e-a968-5d95-8fb6-5d469a5920b9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188040 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y\\begin{vmatrix}x}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)] - μ_{Y\\end{vmatrix}x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 3\\end{bmatrix} &= [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 3)] - μ_{Y \\mid 3}^{2} \\\\\n&= [(1)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + (2)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + (4)^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}] - (\\frac{8}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} + 8 - \\frac{64}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188041 1069 "e-10306" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188041 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{16}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X \\, & \\, Y=2\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $\\mu_{X \\mid 2}=\\dfrac{10}{9}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188041 1072 #uuid "49a039ae-dbe1-5faa-a9a7-014bd9549c03" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188041 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(X - μ_{X \\mid y})^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X\\begin{vmatrix}y}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(x)x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)] - μ_{X\\end{vmatrix}y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$, let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y$, corresponding to the column totals:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{16}$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $\\dfrac{9}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{16}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\nf_{X \\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y) = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_Y(y)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y{:}$\n\n| $f_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\,y)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{8}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{4}{7}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{7}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = 2\\end{bmatrix} &= [∑_(x)x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)] - μ_{X \\mid 2}^{2} \\\\\n&= [1^{2} \\cdot \\frac{8}{9} + 2^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9}] - (\\frac{10}{9})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{9} + \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{100}{81} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{81}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188042 1069 "e-10322" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188042 1071 "What is $9\\times 8 = 72$ expressed as an equivalent division problem?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188042 1072 #uuid "d423a7ca-2274-5ef0-95c7-8295e5f33407" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188042 1074 "Our multiplication problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{9}}\\times {\\color{blue}{8}} = 72\n$$\n\nThere are two division problems equivalent to this multiplication problem:\n\n- ${\\color{red}{9}} = 72\\div {\\color{blue}{8}}$\n- ${\\color{blue}{8}} = 72\\div {\\color{red}{9}}$\n\nWhen writing down a division problem, we usually put the result on the right-hand side. Therefore, our equivalent problems are as follows:\n\n- $72\\div 8 = 9$\n- $72\\div 9 = 8$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188043 1069 "e-10323" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188043 1071 "What is $45\\div 5 = 9$ expressed as an equivalent multiplication problem?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188043 1072 #uuid "0a367fc5-3f32-5bd0-8a78-0e351369039c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188043 1074 "Our division problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{45}}\\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = 9\n$$\n\nThere are two multiplication problems equivalent to the given division problem:\n\n- ${\\color{red}{45}} = {\\color{blue}{5}} \\times 9$\n- ${\\color{red}{45}} = 9 \\times {\\color{blue}{5}}$\n\nWhen writing down a multiplication problem, we usually put the result on the right-hand side. Therefore, our equivalent problems are as follows:\n\n- $5 \\times 9 = 45$\n- $9 \\times 5 = 45$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188044 1069 "e-10324" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188044 1071 "Which of the following division problems are equivalent to the multiplication problem $9\\times 6 = 54$?\n\n1. $54\\div 6 = 9$\n2. $9\\div 6 = 54$\n3. $9\\div 54 = 6$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188044 1072 #uuid "65d1c7af-7d5d-5bc6-820c-9235751a03eb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188044 1074 "Our multiplication problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{9}}\\times {\\color{blue}{6}} = 54\n$$\n\nThere are two division problems equivalent to this multiplication problem:\n\n- ${\\color{red}{9}} = 54\\div {\\color{blue}{6}}$\n- ${\\color{blue}{6}} = 54\\div {\\color{red}{9}}$\n\nWhen writing down a division problem, we usually put the result on the right-hand side. Therefore, our equivalent problems are as follows:\n\n- $54\\div 6 = 9$\n- $54\\div 9 = 6$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188045 1069 "e-10325" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188045 1071 "Which of the following multiplication problems are equivalent to the division problem $120\\div 12 = 10$?\n\n1. $12\\times 10 = 120$\n2. $120\\times 12 = 10$\n3. $10\\times 12 = 120$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188045 1072 #uuid "36d45f8b-d876-5169-90a5-d31093745ef2" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188045 1074 "Our division problem is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{120}}\\div {\\color{blue}{12}} = 10\n$$\n\nThere are two multiplication problems equivalent to the given division problem:\n\n- ${\\color{red}{120}} = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\times 10$\n- ${\\color{red}{120}} = 10 \\times {\\color{blue}{12}}$\n\nWhen writing down a multiplication problem, we usually put the result on the right-hand side. Therefore, our equivalent problems are as follows:\n\n- $12\\times 10 = 120$\n- $10\\times 12 = 120$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188046 1069 "e-10330" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188046 1071 "Write $5,860$ in terms of tens." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188046 1072 #uuid "fbe4353c-5fb2-55ad-be6f-bdae767298e5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188046 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\n5,860 = 586 \\times 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5,860$ is the same as $586$ tens." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188047 1069 "e-10331" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188047 1071 "What is $99,000$ in terms of thousands?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188047 1072 #uuid "64be11f3-dfe6-5cb8-8a0b-3fafd11a029b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188047 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\n99,000 = 99 \\times 1,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $99,000$ is the same as $99$ thousands." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188048 1069 "e-10339" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188048 1071 "Use the box model below to find the **remainder** of $43 \\div 3$.\n\n|  | $10$ | $0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$$3$$-$$0$$0$$0$$0$ | $0$$0$$-$$0$$0$$0$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188048 1072 #uuid "3f9aafd7-648e-5be1-9eab-026bb86a5388" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188048 1074 "First, we subtract $3 \\times {\\color{blue}10}$ from $43$.\n\n|  | $\\color{blue}10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$$3$$-$$3$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$ |  |\n\nNext, we bring $\\color{red}13$ to the right.\n\n|  | $10$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$$3$$-$$3$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$ |\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n3 \\times {\\color{blue}4} = 12\n$$\n\nis less than $13$, we subtract $3 \\times {\\color{blue}4}$ from $13$.\n\n|  | $10$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $4$$3$$-$$3$$0$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$$-$$1$$2$$\\fbox{1}$ |\n\nWe can't subtract any further since $\\fbox{1}$ is less than $3$. Hence, $1$ is the remainder." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188049 1069 "e-10340" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188049 1071 "${3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X \\, & \\, Y=-2\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, -2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{X \\mid -2}=\\dfrac{3}{4}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188049 1072 #uuid "ea6a79c4-09fc-5ae9-9e92-e7bacd4939d9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188049 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(X - μ_{X \\mid y})^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X \\mid y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}X^2 \\, & \\, Y=y \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x^2 \\, f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid - 2)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}3x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{3x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y =-2\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X \\mid - 2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - (\\frac{3}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} - \\frac{9}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{80}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188050 1069 "e-10341" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188050 1071 "${\\frac{3}{y^{4}},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=3\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{Y \\mid 3}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188050 1072 #uuid "fef21fcb-bab6-5f33-a792-280e28a97f81" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188050 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y^2 \\, & \\, X=x \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 3)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{∞}y^{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{y^{4}}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{3}{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{∞}3y^{-2}dy \\\\\n&= [3 \\cdot (y^{-1})/((-1))]_{1}^{∞} \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{3}{y}]_{1}^{∞} \\\\\n&= 0 - (-3) \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 3\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 3\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid 3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 - (\\frac{3}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 - \\frac{9}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188051 1069 "e-10342" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188051 1071 "$f(x, y) = {2y\\ln (x),\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le e, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{X}(x) = {\\ln (x),\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le e, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\,\\big & \\, X=1 \\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that the corresponding expected value is $\\mu_{Y \\mid 1}=\\dfrac{2}{3}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188051 1072 #uuid "acb4923c-ce04-5cd5-af01-2f078a18ceed" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188051 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y^2 \\, & \\, X=x \\big\\end{bmatrix} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y^2 \\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X = x \\big\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$.\n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x) &= (f(x, y))/(f_{X}(x)) \\\\\n&= (2y\\ln (x))/(\\ln (x)) \\\\\n&= 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq e, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1\n$$\n\nOutside this domain, we have\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X} (y \\, \\mid \\, x) = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that $f_{Y \\mid X} (y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ does not depend on $x$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 1)dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot 2ydy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}2y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{4}}{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y \\mid 1}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - (\\frac{2}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{4}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188052 1069 "e-10352" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188052 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\textrm{Var}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}Y \\, & \\, X=1\\big\\end{bmatrix}$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $\\mu_{Y \\mid 1} = \\dfrac{9}{4}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188052 1072 #uuid "3764cb54-7fd2-584a-9dc7-eda88368b032" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188052 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y\\begin{vmatrix}x}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)] - μ_{Y\\end{vmatrix}x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$, let's first find the marginal distribution for $X$, corresponding to the row totals:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x) = \\dfrac{f(x,y)}{f_X(x)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x{:}$\n\n| $f_{Y \\mid X}(y \\, \\mid \\, x)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 1\\end{bmatrix} &= [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 1)] - μ_{Y \\mid 1}^{2} \\\\\n&= [1^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 2^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 3^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}] - (\\frac{9}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + 1 + \\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{81}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188053 1069 "e-10354" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188053 1071 "A customer service department of a large retailer surveys a population of $1800$ customers about their experience with recent purchases. For which of the following situations is it necessary to use a finite population correction factor to adjust the variance in the distribution of the sample mean?\n\n1. They select a sample of $n=75$ customers, and each customer is interviewed once.\n2. They select a sample of $n=100$ customers, and each customer is interviewed once.\n3. They select a sample of $n=150$ customers. For data protection reasons, they decided not to keep track of the customers they interviewed so that each customer could be interviewed more than once.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$ are both known.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188053 1072 #uuid "cce2a080-19c7-51dd-bb39-67b2dda43d70" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188053 1074 "Suppose that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe typically use this approximation when\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\nHowever, if the population size is not large compared to the sample size, the sample data is not independent. To account for this, we add a correction factor to the variance of the sample mean distribution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the population size.\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis called the finite population correction factor, and it is appropriate to use it when $n > 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample includes more than $5\\%$ of the entire population).\n\nNote that if a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nN = 1800, \\quad 5\\%\\cdot N = 90\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation. Notice that\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\nin all cases, so the central limit theorem applies.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n$n|= 75≯90.\\cdot$\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overline{\\,X}$ using a normal distribution, but the finite population correction factor is unnecessary.\n- In situation II, we have\n$n|= 100 > 90.✓$\nAdditionally, the sample is conducted without replacement (since each customer is interviewed only once). Therefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overline{\\,X}$ using a normal distribution with the finite population correction factor\n$\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} = \\dfrac{1800 - 100}{1800 - 1} \\approx 0.945$.\n- In situation III, the finite population correction factor is unnecessary because the sample is independent (since each customer can be interviewed more than once). Therefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overline{\\,X}$ using a normal distribution, but the finite population correction factor is not necessary.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188054 1069 "e-10388" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188054 1071 "Which of the following numbers does **not** have $12$ as a multiple?\n\n1. $6$\n2. $4$\n3. $10$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188054 1072 #uuid "25249b9b-6d90-5cf9-a64c-64f37b3d0dce" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188054 1074 "Every whole number is a multiple of its factors. For example, the following two statements are equivalent (mean the same thing):\n\n- ${\\color{blue}{18}}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$\n- ${\\color{red}{3}}$ is a factor of ${\\color{blue}{18}}$\n\nFirst, we write down the factor pairs of $12$:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n12 = 1 \\times 12\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n12 = 2 \\times 6\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n12 = 3 \\times 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the factors of $12$ are as follows:\n$1, \\quad 2, \\quad 3, \\quad 4, \\quad 6, \\quad 12$\n\nEach of these numbers has $12$ as a multiple.\n\nFrom the given options, the only number that is not on our list above is $10$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188055 1069 "e-10391" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188055 1071 "Which of the following numbers has $11$ as a multiple?\n\n1. $8$\n2. $11$\n3. $22$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188055 1072 #uuid "4012769b-60e4-5965-9cf3-d8e3c4abc89c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188055 1074 "Every whole number is a multiple of its factors. For example, the following two statements are equivalent (mean the same thing):\n\n- ${\\color{blue}{18}}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$\n- ${\\color{red}{3}}$ is a factor of ${\\color{blue}{18}}$\n\nNotice that $11$ is prime. Therefore, it has only two factors, namely $1$ and itself:\n$1,\\quad 11$\n\nFrom the given options, the only number that is in our list above is $11$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188056 1069 "e-10395" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188056 1071 "$\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\square \\,\\dfrac 1 2$\n\nDetermine which of the following symbols could replace the empty box above to make the statement true.\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188056 1072 #uuid "8be5c0a0-ea0a-5593-9dd4-b857850382a4" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188056 1074 "When comparing fractions to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nwe recall the following:\n\n- A fraction is *greater than* $\\dfrac12$ if its numerator is *greater than* one-half of its denominator.\n- A fraction is *less than* $\\dfrac12$ if its numerator is *less than* one-half of its denominator.\n\nWe wish to compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the numerator is ${\\color{red}{3}}$,\n- the denominator is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$,\n- one-half of the denominator is ${\\color{blue}{4}} \\div 2 = {\\color{purple}{2}}$.\n\nSo, the numerator is *greater than* one-half of the denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} > {\\color{purple}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{3}}{\\color{blue}{4}} > \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nWe can also represent this comparison using a fraction model:\n\n![](images/4b/4b4a3e06167dd24a640e8f2963dd93dd190bf31ba4aaa1cbd4488f98bb371a5d.png)\n\nThe fraction on the left has $\\color{blue}3$ shaded parts, while the fraction on the right has $\\color{purple}2$ shaded parts. Therefore, the fraction on the left is larger." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188057 1069 "e-10396" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188057 1071 "Which of the following fractions is **NOT** equivalent to $\\dfrac12$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{4}{8}$\n2. $\\dfrac{6}{12}$\n3. $\\dfrac{6}{8}$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188057 1072 #uuid "c437808d-8574-55fe-82a6-2f89b6da2e5c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188057 1074 "A fraction is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nif its numerator is one-half of its denominator.\n\nFrom the given options, the only fraction where the numerator is **not** one-half of its denominator is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\div 2 = 4 \\neq {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\neq\n$$\n\nmeans \"is not equal to.\"\n\nWe can also represent this visually using a fraction model.\n\n![](images/d8/d81850be64340cda34a20b3da69e1b99fb60c7c285128c067ae9679b2877bf87.png)\n\nNotice that the shaded areas are *not* the same. Therefore, the fractions are *not* equal." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188058 1069 "e-10406" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188058 1071 "$\\dfrac 89 \\,\\square \\,\\dfrac 76$\n\nUsing a benchmark of $1$, determine which symbols could replace the empty box above to make the statement true.\n\n1. $>$\n2. $=$\n3. $<$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188058 1072 #uuid "c16ad61f-8806-5a26-9a05-4562e4a5cf83" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188058 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- For the fraction $\\dfrac{8}{9}$, the numerator $(8)$ is *smaller than* the denominator $(9)$. It is a proper fraction, so it must be *smaller than* $1$.\n- For the fraction $\\dfrac{7}{6}$, the numerator $(7)$ is *larger than* the denomionator $(6)$. It is an improper fraction, so it must be *larger than* $1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 8 9 < \\dfrac 7 6\n$$\n\nWe can also deduce this from a fraction model:\n\n![](images/9b/9bdf49ae41d288867faf00a77b60ec6c5e89de137d6d5c4ea9d0837e0f20dfd7.png)\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188059 1069 "e-10407" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188059 1071 "$\\dfrac{3}{4} \\,\\square \\,\\dfrac{4}{5}$\n\nUsing a benchmark of $1$, determine which symbols could replace the empty box above to make the statement true.\n\n1. $<$\n2. $=$\n3. $>$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188059 1072 #uuid "6347698e-36b5-561f-92e4-4ee8aadb1a13" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188059 1074 "Notice that the numerator is one less than the denominator for both fractions. In other words, each fraction is one piece short of a whole.\n\nSince fifths are smaller than quarters,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nis further from one whole than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis smaller than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac45{.}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ff7d437d19549f192f4e84cf3c926283ff8e895fba81a693a3bba67cf1ddee49.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} < \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188060 1069 "e-10417" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188060 1071 "According to the equation $7 \\times 9 = 63$, how many times larger is $63$ than $9$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188060 1072 #uuid "f22c1b49-037d-5614-b8f5-a6445baef63c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188060 1074 "The given equation compares the size of the product $(63)$ to its factors $(7$ and $9)$. It can be interpreted as a comparison in the following two ways:\n\n- \"*The number $63$ is $7$ times larger than $9$.*\"\n- \"*The number $63$ is $9$ times larger than $7$.*\"\n\nTherefore, the answer is $7$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188061 1069 "e-10419" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188061 1071 "Which of the following statements is represented by the equation $7 \\times 2 = 14$?\n\n1. The number $14$ is $2$ larger than $7$.\n2. The number $14$ is $2$ times larger than $7$.\n3. The number $7$ is $2$ times larger than $14$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188061 1072 #uuid "c89e980b-2f72-569f-8767-6a744f9fe7a3" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188061 1074 "The given equation compares the size of the product $(14)$ to its factors $(2$ and $7)$. It can be interpreted as a comparison in the following two ways:\n\n- \"*The number $14$ is $7$ **times** larger than $2$.*\"\n- \"*The number $14$ is $2$ **times** larger than $7$.*\"\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the correct answer is \"*The number $14$ is $2$ times larger than $7$.*\"\n\n**Watch Out!** The word \"times\" is important. The following answer is **not** correct.\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number $14$ is $2$ larger than $7$.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188062 1069 "e-10420" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188062 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.352, \\quad 0.567, \\quad 0.801$\n\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the CDF $F(x)$, given by\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1, \\\\ 2 - \\frac{2}{x},\\end{vmatrix}1 \\le x \\le 2,; 1, \\mid x > 2$.\n\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188062 1072 #uuid "d57db364-7120-581a-a402-af8aaf3b6d92" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188062 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nSo, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. To do this, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2 - \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\ny - 2 &= -\\frac{2}{x} \\\\\n2 - y &= \\frac{2}{x} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{2 - y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.352$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.352} \\approx 1.214$\n- For $y = 0.567$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.567} \\approx 1.396$\n- For $y = 0.801$, we get $x = \\dfrac{2}{2 - 0.801} \\approx 1.668$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188063 1069 "e-10421" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188063 1071 "The number $72$ is $9$ times larger than which number?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188063 1072 #uuid "03d625db-478b-5218-ab4b-f8d3515c5497" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188063 1074 "Let's examine our statement, highlighting some important words:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number $72$ is $9$ **times larger** than which number?*\n\nSo, the statement compares the size of a *product* $(72)$ to its *factors,* $9$, and ${\\color{blue}{n}}$. Here, ${\\color{blue}{n}}$ represents the unknown second factor.\n\nTherefore, our statement can be interpreted in the following two ways:\n\n$$\n9\\times {\\color{blue}{n}} = 72, \\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad {\\color{blue}{n}}\\times 9= 72\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{n}} = {\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n9\\times {\\color{blue}{8}} = 72, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{8}}\\times 9=72\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{n}} = {\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188064 1069 "e-10422" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188064 1071 "What number is $9$ times larger than $8$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188064 1072 #uuid "c6a04dcb-f75b-5156-89d8-39ab39a9d337" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188064 1074 "Let's examine our question statement, highlighting some important words:\n\n$\\qquad$ *What number is $9$ **times larger** than $8$?*\n\nSo, the statement compares the size of a *product* $({\\color{blue}{n}})$ to its *factors* $(8$ and $9)$. Here, the letter ${\\color{blue}{n}}$ represents the unknown product.\n\nTherefore, our statement can be interpreted in the following two ways:\n\n$$\n9\\times 8 = {\\color{blue}{n}}, \\qquad \\textrm{or}\\qquad 8 \\times 9 = {\\color{blue}{n}}\n$$\n\nCalculating the products, we have\n\n$$\n9 \\times 8 = {\\color{blue}{72}}, \\qquad 8 \\times 9 = {\\color{blue}{72}}\n$$\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{n}}={\\color{blue}{72}}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188065 1069 "e-10424" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188065 1071 "Hank has $36$ marbles. If Hank has $4$ times as many marbles as Ned, how many marbles does Ned have?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188065 1072 #uuid "5c2fa091-761b-5c52-8658-6d32b7bd6b7b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188065 1074 "Let ${\\color{blue}{N}}$ be the number of marbles Ned has. Now, let's list the given information:\n\n- Hank has $36$ marbles.\n- Hank has $4$ times as many marbles as Ned.\n\nSo, the number of marbles Hank has, $36$, is $4$ times larger than the number of marbles Ned has, ${\\color{blue}{N}}$. This can be represented by the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n4 \\times {\\color{blue}{N}} = 36\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} = 9\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n4 \\times {\\color{blue}{9}} = 36\n$$\n\nSo, Ned has $9$ marbles." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188066 1069 "e-10425" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188066 1071 "Suppose the following random sample was drawn from the uniform distribution $U[0,1]$.\n$0.354, \\quad 0.593, \\quad 0.792$\nUse these numbers to generate three random numbers that follow a probability distribution with the PDF $f(x)$, given by\n${\\cos x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nRound your answers to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188066 1072 #uuid "33cf278d-ed9e-5d09-89eb-1076cc7fad66" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188066 1074 "Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where\n\n$$\nF(a) = 0, F(b) = 1\n$$\n\nand $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a < x < b$. Then, the random variable $X$, given by\n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\nis a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\nFirst, we must find the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ corresponding to our random variable. Since our PDF $f(x)$ is non-negative only for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sin t\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\sin x,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2},; 1, \\mid x > \\frac{π}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we need to find the inverse of $y = F(x)$. So, we solve the following equation for $x$, taking into account that\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $0 \\lt y \\lt 1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\sin x \\\\\nx &= \\arcsin y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the numbers drawn from $U[0,1]$ into the above equation:\n\n- For $y = 0.354$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.354) \\approx 0.362$\n- For $y = 0.593$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.593) \\approx 0.635$\n- For $y = 0.792$, we get $x = \\arcsin(0.792) \\approx 0.914$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188067 1069 "e-10426" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188067 1071 "Olivia wants to pack $8$ toy blocks in a box. Each toy block weighs $42\\,\\textrm{g}$ and the box weighs $214\\,\\textrm{g}$. What is the total weight of the box with the blocks inside?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188067 1072 #uuid "5e3922ea-1a3e-50b0-9860-b2456006b2e5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188067 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- There are $8$ toy blocks.\n- Each toy block weighs $42\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n- The weight of the box is $214\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n\nLet $W$ represent the total weight of the box with the blocks inside. This is the number we want to find.\n\n- There are $8$ toy blocks, and each toy block weighs $42\\,\\textrm{g}$. Therefore, the total weight of the blocks (in grams) is\n$42 \\cdot 8|= 40 \\cdot 8 + 2 \\cdot 8; = 320 + 16; = 336$.\n- The total weight, $W$, equals the weight calculated above $({\\color{blue} 336}\\,\\textrm{g})$ plus the weight of the box $({\\color{red}214}\\,\\textrm{g})$. Therefore, the total weight of the box with the blocks inside (in grams) is\n$W = {\\color{blue} 336} + {\\color{red} 214}$.\n\nCalculating the final sum, we get\n\n$$\nW = 336 + 214 = 550\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the box with the blocks inside is $550\\,\\textrm{g}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188068 1069 "e-10427" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188068 1071 "A chemistry teacher has $9$ vials, each containing $21\\,\\textrm{ml}$ of acid solution. He gives one vial to each of his $8$ students to perform an experiment. Each student uses $10\\,\\textrm{ml}$ of the acid solution. What is the total amount of acid solution left after the experiment?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188068 1072 #uuid "1bd5b4c4-d321-507c-bafe-4936348eb30d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188068 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- Initially, there are $9$ vials, and each vial contains $21\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution.\n- Each of the $8$ students uses $10\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution.\n\nLet $A$ represent the total amount of acid solution left after the experiment. This is the number we want to find.\n\n- Initially, there are $21\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution in each of the $9$ vials. Therefore, the initial amount (in milliliters) of the acid solution is\n$21 \\cdot 9|= 20 \\cdot 9 + 1 \\cdot 9; = 180 + 9; = 189$.\n- Each of the $8$ students uses $10\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution. Therefore, the amount (in milliliters) of acid solution used during the experiment is\n$8 \\cdot 10|= 80$.\n\nTo find the total amount of acid solution left after the experiment, we subtract our two values:\n\n$$\nA = {\\color{red}{189}} - {\\color{blue}{80}} = 109\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $109\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution left after the experiment." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188069 1069 "e-10433" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188069 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ a & 5 & b \\\\ c & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ for some random variables $X_1, X_2$, and $X_3$ is given above. Find $a + b + c$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188069 1072 #uuid "c72f3cc2-5851-5c6b-8edc-74377b88055a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188069 1074 "For random variables $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X_{1}] & Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{1}, X_{n}] \\\\ Cov[X_{2}, X_{1}] & Var[X_{2}] & ⋯ & Cov[X_{2}, X_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ Cov[X_{n}, X_{1}] & Cov[X_{n}, X_{2}] & ⋯ & Var[X_{n}]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is $\\Sigma_{ii} = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]$, and\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i \\neq j$ is $\\Sigma_{ij} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]$.\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{Cov}[Y,X]\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix).\n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a = \\Sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = \\Sigma_{12} = 2$\n- $b = \\Sigma_{23} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_2] = \\Sigma_{32} = 1$\n- $c = \\Sigma_{31} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_3,X_1] = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = \\Sigma_{13} = 0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na + b + c = 2 + 1 +0 = 3\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188070 1069 "e-10434" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188070 1071 "Write the number $81$ in words." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188070 1072 #uuid "6ccd8665-36f4-597c-8529-31aaa2487e23" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188070 1074 "To write a two-digit number (greater than $20$) in words, we\n\n- write the tens part, followed by a hyphen\n- then, write the ones part.\n\nLet's write down the place values of $81$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}8$ | $\\color{red}1$ |\n\nNow, we write the number in words:\n\n- The number has $\\color{blue}8$ tens, so we write \"eighty,\" followed by a hyphen:$\\qquad$\"*eighty-*\"\n- The number has $\\color{red}1$ ones, so we add \"one\": $\\qquad$ \"*eighty-one*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $81$ written in words is *eighty-one*." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188071 1069 "e-10435" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188071 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 0.24$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.2$. Determine $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y]$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188071 1072 #uuid "7ea98738-258e-5cc4-9e04-58b0b4638290" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188071 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X \\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X - Y] = 3 + 0.24 - 2 \\cdot 0.2 = 2.84\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188072 1069 "e-10436" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188072 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, for $(x,y) \\in [0,1] \\times [0,2]$, is given by\n\n$F(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy}{8}(2x^2+y)$.\n\nFind $F_X(x)$, the marginal CDF of $X$, for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188072 1072 #uuid "731d3a2d-4853-52e4-9c86-3654dc66adab" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188072 1074 "The marginal CDF of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF_X(x) = F(x,\\infty) = \\lim\\limits_{y \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_{X}(x) &= F(x, ∞) \\\\\n&= F(x, 2) \\\\\n&= (x(2))/(8)(2x^{2} + (2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4x}{8}(x^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(x^{3} + x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188073 1069 "e-10443" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188073 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 5$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 2$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 3$. Find $\\textrm{Var}[2X - 5Y]$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188073 1072 #uuid "0bc5206c-e880-5016-9653-49ac54af4d2f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188073 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[2X - 5Y] &= 2^{2} \\cdot 5 + (-5)^{2} \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-5) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 20 + 50 - 60 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188074 1069 "e-10444" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188074 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y]$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188074 1072 #uuid "04bff442-bf0b-529b-a8d8-14538a1db48f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188074 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. If $X$ is defined on a sample space $S_X$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.4 + 3 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x^{2} \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 5.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.4 - 1.8^{2} \\\\\n&= 2.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space $S_Y$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.5 + 2 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y^{2} \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.5 + 2^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(0, 0) + 0 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(0, 2) + 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(3, 0) + 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(3, 2) \\\\\n&= 0 + 0 + 0 + 6 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[XY] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 1.2 - 1.8 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[5X + 2Y] &= 5^{2} \\cdot 2.16 + 2^{2} \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-0.6) \\\\\n&= 54 + 4 - 12 \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188075 1069 "e-10447" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188075 1071 "For three random variables $X_1,X_2$, and $X_3$, we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$\\textrm{Var}[X_1] = 1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_2] = 3, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_3] = 8 \\qquad$\n\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = 2, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = 4, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = -5 \\qquad$\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X_1 + 2X_2 + 3X_3]$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188075 1072 #uuid "d849af65-721a-5a47-b8b7-9446e77046f2" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188075 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, and $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i < j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j]\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[a_{1}X_{1} + a_{2}X_{2} + a_{3}X_{3}] &= a_{1}^{2}Var[X_{1}] + a_{2}^{2}Var[X_{2}] + a_{3}^{2}Var[X_{3}] \\\\\n&= + 2a_{1}a_{2}Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] + 2a_{1}a_{3}Cov[X_{1}, X_{3}] + 2a_{2}a_{3}Cov[X_{2}, X_{3}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[5X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 3X_{3}] &= 5^{2} \\cdot 1 + 2^{2} \\cdot 3 + 3^{2} \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-5) \\\\\n&= 25 + 12 + 72 + 40 + 120 - 60 \\\\\n&= 209\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188076 1069 "e-10452" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188076 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 3$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.2$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188076 1072 #uuid "5b315157-43c6-5b8e-ac70-8b781b41cec9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188076 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. If $X$ is defined on a support $S_X$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x^{2} \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot 0.5 + 4^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 10 - 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support $S_Y$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y^{2} \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 1^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 5.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.8 - 2.2^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\textrm{E}[XY]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= (2)(1)f(2, 1) + (4)(1)f(4, 1) + (2)(3)f(2, 3) + (4)(3)f(4, 3) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0.3 + 4 \\cdot 0.1 + 6 \\cdot 0.2 + 12 \\cdot 0.4 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 7 - 3 \\cdot 2.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188077 1069 "e-10453" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188077 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4$, $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 3$, mixed moment $\\textrm{E}[XY] = 9$, and second raw moments $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = 18$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y^2] = 14$. Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188077 1072 #uuid "33336c58-c83a-512f-8a79-9223d1442763" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188077 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\textrm{Var}[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 18 - 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 14 - 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 9 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188078 1069 "e-10454" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188078 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n${x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nIf $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}$, $\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac{5}{8}$, and $\\textrm{E}[XY]=\\dfrac{17}{48}$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188078 1072 #uuid "3a22ccc6-495b-5b89-8b7d-775e6e620fe0" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188078 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\nWe compute $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2} \\cdot f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}(x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{3} + \\frac{3}{2}x^{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{4}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{3}y^{2}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{2}y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{4}y + \\frac{1}{6}y^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12} - (\\frac{7}{12})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{144}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\textrm{E}[Y^2]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}y^{2}(x + \\frac{3}{2}y^{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}(xy^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y^{4})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}xy^{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}y^{2} + \\frac{3}{2}y^{4})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}y^{3} + \\frac{3}{10}y^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{3}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{15} - (\\frac{5}{8})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{73}{960}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{17}{48} - (\\frac{7}{12}) \\cdot (\\frac{5}{8}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{96}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Var[X] & Cov[X, Y] \\\\ Cov[Y, X] & Var[Y]\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188079 1069 "e-10500" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188079 1071 "![](images/5a/5a17d33b3acd6c711f5ba6326f63304d19b7a576c3cb1840fa2e553829738469.png)\n\nWhich of the above are decompositions of the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{4}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188079 1072 #uuid "62dc3a18-0a3d-591a-8c90-b727bb598815" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188079 1074 "Let's examine each model in turn.\n\n- I is a decomposition of $\\dfrac{3}{4}$. Indeed, each fraction model contains $4$ parts, and the total number of shaded parts equals $3$.\n\n![](images/0a/0a06dc7906f0b3657d6eb768b1534945ee6b3870a3331058a0534474a105d13f.png)\n\n- II is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{3}{4}$. Each fraction model contains $4$ parts. But the total number of shaded parts equals $5$, not $3$. As a result, this cannot be a decomposition of a fraction with a numerator of $3$.\n\n- III is *not* a decomposition of $\\dfrac{3}{4}$. Notice that each fraction model contains $6$ parts. As a result, this cannot be a decomposition of a fraction with a denominator of $4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188080 1069 "e-10501" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188080 1071 "What is the decomposition of $\\dfrac{5}{3}$ shown in the diagram below?\n\n![](images/3a/3a40da4e6a283b5218335ef8e3cddf3dddfc75db81c92fbe00b8b9f2b612ca1d.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188080 1072 #uuid "babd6e70-26c7-52f4-8bf7-1dbe735536ee" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188080 1074 "Notice that the line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts. So, each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n- Starting from zero, the $1$st jump covers $2$ parts. So, it corresponds to the fraction $\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}3}$.\n- The $2$nd jump covers $1$ part. So, it corresponds to the fraction $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}$.\n- The $3$rd jump covers $2$ parts. So, it corresponds to the fraction $\\dfrac{2}{\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nIn total, there are\n\n$$\n2+1+2={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\njumps. So, the resulting fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d16c329188b81158473a40ae7df2fe52bbbcd9330f7c49c917e9ec2abbb9eaa.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} + \\dfrac{1}{3} + \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188081 1069 "e-10506" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188081 1071 "What is the decomposition of $\\dfrac{4}{6}$ shown in the diagram below?\n\n![](images/dc/dceb81e9f38f79d579d634fde8239c81fbf1a3729f85c0f1340ba813bdf34f1d.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188081 1072 #uuid "063679b0-bb02-5abb-bcb1-474352bf892d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188081 1074 "The diagram represents one whole, and there are $6$ small rectangles. Therefore, each small rectangle represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c61ada9be148813f93e8d6faf121829b0f5e10672f975e6013117aa590096fd.png)\n\nAs a result:\n\n- the $1$st fraction above the tape is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$\n- the $2$nd fraction above the tape is $\\dfrac{2}{6}$\n- the $3$rd fraction above the tape is $\\dfrac{1}{6}$\n\nThese three fractions add up to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac{2}{6} + \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188082 1069 "e-10579" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188082 1071 "Which of the following equations shows that $12$ is $8$ smaller than $20$?\n\n1. $20- 8= 12$\n2. $20 + 8= 12$\n3. $20 \\div 8 = 12$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188082 1072 #uuid "3cdbdb2c-33e9-5202-830f-dd4c768e5a35" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188082 1074 "Let's examine our statement, highlighting some important words:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Which of the following equations shows that $12$ is $8$ **smaller than** $20$?*\n\nSo, the statement compares the size of the number $12$ to the *difference* of $20$ and $8$. It can be interpreted as a comparison in the following way:\n\n$$\n20 - 8= 12\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188083 1069 "e-10580" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188083 1071 "Eve and Rosie went shopping. If Eve spent $5$ times as much as Rosie and, in total, she spent $\\[math]105$, what equation could we use to determine how much Rosie spent? Use $R$ to represent the amount of money Rosie spent." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188083 1072 #uuid "e16f5c50-7ab3-547b-a74c-55b2890adea1" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188083 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important parts of the given information:\n\n- Eve spent $\\[math]105$.\n- Eve spent $5$ times as much as Rosie.\n\nSo, the amount spent by Eve, $\\[/math]105$, is $5$ **times larger** than the amount spent by Rosie, $R$. This can be represented by the following multiplication problems:\n\n$$\nR \\times 5 = 105 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 5 \\times R = 105\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188084 1069 "e-10581" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188084 1071 "There were $B$ boys and $36$ girls at a summer camp. If there were $9$ more girls than boys at the camp, which equation could be used to find how many boys there were?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188084 1072 #uuid "dcc8fa8a-6262-56d8-afd6-9b0765906374" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188084 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important parts of the given information:\n\n- There were $B$ boys at a summer camp.\n- There were also $36$ girls at the camp, and this was $9$ more than the number of boys.\n\nSo, the number of girls, $36$, is $9$ **larger** than the number of boys, $B$. This can be represented by the following addition problems:\n\n$$\nB+9 = 36 \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad 9 + B = 36\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188085 1069 "e-10582" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188085 1071 "On Monday, Nicholas ate $3$ donuts. On Tuesday, he ate $4$ more donuts than on Monday. If each donut weighs $28\\,\\textrm{g}$, how many grams of donuts did Nicholas eat on Tuesday?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188085 1072 #uuid "ea9b7804-8c88-5930-bc58-30f0c369ca0b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188085 1074 "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- On Monday, Nicholas ate $3$ donuts.\n- On Tuesday, he ate $4$ more donuts than on Monday.\n- Each donut weighs $28\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n\nLet $W$ be the weight of the donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday, which is the number we want to find.\n\n- The number of donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday is $4$ *more* than the number of donuts he ate on Monday $(3)$. Therefore, the number of donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday is\n$4 + 3 = {\\color{blue}7}$.\n- Nicholas ate ${\\color{blue}7}$ donuts on Tuesday, and each donut weighs $28\\,\\textrm{g}$. Therefore, the weight of the donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday (in grams) is\n$W|= 28 \\cdot 7; = 20 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot 7; = 140 + 56; = 196$.\n\nTherefore, on Tuesday, Nicholas ate $196\\,\\textrm{g}$ of donuts." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188086 1069 "e-12845" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188086 1071 "How is \"five hundred thirty-two\" written using digits?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188086 1072 #uuid "501799d2-fcc1-510b-8324-0c8cf13f4925" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188086 1074 "First, we break down the number \"five hundred thirty-two\":\n\n- \"five hundred\" means the number has $\\color{blue}5$ hundreds\n- \"thirty\" means the number has $\\color{red}3$ tens\n- \"two\" means the number has $\\color{purple}2$ ones\n\nNow, we write the values in a place value chart:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}5$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{purple}2$ |\n\nTherefore, \"five hundred thirty-two\" written using digits is $532$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188087 1069 "e-12846" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188087 1071 "How is \"four hundred one thousand, twenty\" written using digits?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188087 1072 #uuid "3fb0f080-c509-50dc-bc6d-da71663ebcba" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188087 1074 "Let's start by splitting the number into periods. To do this, we split the sentence on either side of \"thousands\":\n\n$\\qquad$ \"four hundred one thousand, twenty\"\n\nNow, we write each period using digits:\n\n- The first period \"four hundred one\" has $4$ hundreds, $0$ tens, and $1$ one. So, the first period is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{401}$.\n- The second period \"twenty\" has $0$ hundreds, $2$ tens, and $0$ ones. So, the second period is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{020}$.\n\nCombining the first and second periods, separated by a comma, we get\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{401},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{020}$.\n\nTherefore, \"four hundred one thousand, twenty\" written using digits is $401,020$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188088 1069 "e-12847" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188088 1071 "How is \"thirty-nine million, one hundred one thousand, two\" written using digits?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188088 1072 #uuid "4df35740-4039-5d0d-bdd1-88a8ffaab5d9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188088 1074 "Let's start by splitting the number into periods. To do this, we split the sentence to either side of \"millions\" and \"thousands\":\n\n$\\qquad$ \"thirty-nine million, one hundred one thousand, two\"\n\nNow, we write each period using digits:\n\n- The first period \"thirty-nine\" has $3$ tens and $9$ ones. So, the first period is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{39}$.\n- The second period \"one hundred one\" has $1$ hundred, $0$ tens, and $1$ one. So, the second period is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{101}$.\n- The third period \"two\" has $0$ hundreds, $0$ tens, and $2$ ones. So, the third period is $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{002}$.\n\nCombining the first, second, and third periods, separated by commas, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{39},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{101},\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{002}$.\n\nTherefore, \"thirty-nine million, one hundred one thousand, two\" written using digits is $39,101,002$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188089 1069 "e-12888" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188089 1071 "What is $36\\div 6 \\times 10$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188089 1072 #uuid "528d580f-a39c-5374-a08f-49a3373f2256" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188089 1074 "When the only operations are multiplication and division, we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 \\div 6 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(36 \\div 6) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n6 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188090 1069 "e-12889" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188090 1071 "Compute $2\\div 8 \\times 12$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188090 1072 #uuid "d3d4b515-dec0-5cba-8ca5-524fe1ec0ad7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188090 1074 "When the only operations are multiplication and division,\n\n- we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer, and\n- we can swap the order in which the operations are carried out.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\div 8 \\cdot 12 &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 12 \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 12) \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n24 \\div 8 &=  \\\\\n&= 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188091 1069 "e-12890" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188091 1071 "Find $24\\times 5 \\div 3$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188091 1072 #uuid "6299c6bd-1577-5c57-8782-0a31bf1ce877" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188091 1074 "When the only operations are multiplication and division,\n\n- we carry out the operations from left to right, using parentheses $(\\cdots)$ to make our working clearer, and\n- we can swap the order in which the operations are carried out.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 \\cdot 5 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n24 \\div 3 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n(24 \\div 3) \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 5 &=  \\\\\n&= 40 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188092 1069 "e-13094" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188092 1071 "Simplify the expression $-3-(5+2u)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188092 1072 #uuid "7f43385c-ccc0-5855-916a-e6bb689c290f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188092 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-3 - 5 - 2u\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3 - 5 - 2u &=  \\\\\n(-3 - 5) - 2u &=  \\\\\n&= - 8 - 2u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188093 1069 "e-13095" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188093 1071 "Simplify the expression $11-(5a-7)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188093 1072 #uuid "d73b6911-278d-5c8e-a900-fc751fa518fa" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188093 1074 "First, we remove the parentheses by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n11-5a + 7\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 - 5a + 7 &=  \\\\\n(11 + 7) - 5a &=  \\\\\n&= 18 - 5a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188094 1069 "e-13096" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188094 1071 "Simplify the expression $-9v-4(3+5v)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188094 1072 #uuid "34317825-e142-5f97-951f-45f87d9672d5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188094 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $-9v$ must be expanded using the distributive law.\n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[4(3+5v)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - \\Big[12+20v\\Big]\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n-9v - 12 - 20v\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9v - 12 - 20v &=  \\\\\n(-9v - 20v) - 12 &=  \\\\\n&= - 29v - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188095 1069 "e-13097" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188095 1071 "Simplify the expression $10 - 2(3x - 4)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188095 1072 #uuid "d6283f26-9e20-5255-b358-8bc0c6523065" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188095 1074 "Notice that the expression we need to subtract from $10$ must be expanded using the distributive law.\n\nSo first, let's write some big brackets around the expression in parentheses, keeping the minus sign *outside*.\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[2(3x-4)\\Big]\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the expression inside the brackets using the distributive law. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - \\Big[6x-8\\Big]\n$$\n\nNow, we remove the brackets by distributing the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n10 - 6x + 8\n$$\n\nFinally, we collect like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 - 6x + 8 &=  \\\\\n(10 + 8) - 6x &=  \\\\\n&= 18 - 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188096 1069 "e-13390" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188096 1071 "\"$7$ thousands and $3$ tens\" is equivalent to how many ones?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188096 1072 #uuid "985fd4b5-2bb2-5b43-8cae-e439481fe7d7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188096 1074 "Let's write \"$7$ thousands and $3$ tens\" in a place value chart:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, \"$7$ thousands and $3$ tens\" is equivalent to \"$7,030$ ones.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188097 1069 "e-13391" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188097 1071 "\"$4,700$ ones\" is equivalent to how many thousands and hundreds?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188097 1072 #uuid "b349bd6e-5978-5f3f-a55b-7d3ae22318e5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188097 1074 "Let's write the number $4,700$ in a place value chart:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $7$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, \"$4,700$ ones\" is equivalent to \"$4$ thousands and $7$ hundreds.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188098 1069 "e-13392" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188098 1071 "\"$16$ thousands\" is equivalent to how many ten-thousands and thousands?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188098 1072 #uuid "a912f87b-56d5-5199-a852-ce01c7b88b1c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188098 1074 "Since\n\n$$\n16 = 10+6\n$$\n\nwe can write $16$ thousands as\n\n$$\n10\\,\\textrm{thousands} + 6\\,\\textrm{thousands}\n$$\n\nNow, since $10$ thousands is the same as $1$ ten-thousand, the statement above is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{ten-thousands} + 6\\,\\textrm{thousands}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \"$16$ thousands\" is equivalent to \"$1$ ten-thousand and $6$ thousands\"." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188099 1069 "e-13403" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188099 1071 "There are currently $243$ trees in the park. Workers will plant $158$ more trees this week. By rounding each value to the nearest hundred, estimate how many trees will be in the park when the workers are finished." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188099 1072 #uuid "6477acda-4609-570d-99a8-aaa73a6d0cee" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188099 1074 "To estimate how many trees will be in the park when the workers are finished, we need to find an approximation for the sum\n\n$$\n243+158\n$$\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n243\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 158 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 200 & +\\end{vmatrix}200\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{200}} + {\\color{blue}{200}}$:\n$2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n243 + 158 \\approx 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be approximately $400$ trees in the park when the workers are finished." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188100 1069 "e-13404" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188100 1071 "Estimate the sum $28,170 + 3,802$ by rounding each number to the nearest thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188100 1072 #uuid "43b056ab-64c9-507f-804b-f54627ef1596" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188100 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest thousand:\n\n$$\n28, 170\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 3, 802 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 28, 000 & +\\end{vmatrix}4, 000\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{28,000}} + {\\color{blue}{4,000}}$:\n$1; 2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0; 3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0$\n\nTherefore, $28,170 + 3,802$ is approximately $32,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188101 1069 "e-13405" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188101 1071 "Estimate the sum $788,134 + 2,531,765$ by rounding each number to the nearest hundred thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188101 1072 #uuid "57e81305-eb0c-5a34-ae62-3687f2d1e28c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188101 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred- thousand:\n\n$$\n788, 134\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2, 531, 765 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 800, 000 & +\\end{vmatrix}2, 500, 000\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{800,000}} + {\\color{blue}{2,500,000}}$:\n$1; 8\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0$\nTherefore, $788,134 + 2,531,765$ is approximately $3,300,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188102 1069 "e-13414" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188102 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac 3 7 - \\dfrac 1 7$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188102 1072 #uuid "ee41d6d8-9d1b-5177-a6bf-fba13f296ae8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188102 1074 "To subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{7} - \\frac{1}{7} = \\frac{3 - 1}{7} = \\frac{2}{7}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188103 1069 "e-13415" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188103 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac{5}{6} - \\dfrac{3}{6}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188103 1072 #uuid "411c2102-f8d3-5d96-b2fc-6c307502a92e" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188103 1074 "To subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{5 - 3}{6} = \\frac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} = \\dfrac{2\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} - \\dfrac{3}{6} = \\dfrac 1 3\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188104 1069 "e-13416" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188104 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188104 1072 #uuid "8a3f6094-6721-5c14-8f44-afdbb37f96ba" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188104 1074 "To subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9} = \\dfrac{23 - 8}{9} = \\dfrac{15}{9}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{9} = \\dfrac{15 \\div 3}{9 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n5 \\div 3 = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 2\n$$\n\nwe can write the answer as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{3} = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{23}{9} - \\dfrac{8}{9} = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188105 1069 "e-13422" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188105 1071 "Tom and Jerry are running a race that takes place over several days. Tom runs a total of $122$ kilometers, while Jerry runs $36$ kilometers more than Tom. How many kilometers did Jerry run?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188105 1072 #uuid "fa075521-c9f5-5859-8752-c573dc76be55" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188105 1074 "To find the number of kilometers Jerry ran, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n122 + 36\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ + & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}6; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}6; 5 \\mid 8\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}0} = {\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jerry ran $158$ kilometers." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188106 1069 "e-13424" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188106 1071 "What is $51 + 267$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188106 1072 #uuid "a6f51991-9a42-5c69-a090-7e475700e12e" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188106 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6 \\mid 7\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}7} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6 \\mid 7; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}1}$ below the *tens* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *hundreds*:\n\n$$\n1; 5\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}0} + {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n51 + 267 = 318\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188107 1069 "e-13425" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188107 1071 "What is $89 + 982$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188107 1072 #uuid "8f298048-e248-508e-908e-702632b423fc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188107 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 8 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}9} + {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}1}$ below the *ones* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *tens*:\n\n$$\n1; 8\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 8 \\mid 2; 1\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}8} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}7}$ below the *tens* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *hundreds*:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}9; +\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2; 7 \\mid 1\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}0} + {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}0}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}0}$ below the *hundreds* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *thousands*:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}9; +\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2; 0 \\mid 7 \\mid 1\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Adding the thousands,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1}+{\\color{blue}0}+{\\color{blue}0}={\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n89 + 982 = 1,071\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188108 1069 "e-13429" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188108 1071 "![](images/3f/3ff2c6e79509aa5b09b2c54a87da646843fa0f99b19fc0ab22abb5c71122b8ca.png)\n\nGiven the model above, evaluate $25 \\div 6$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188108 1072 #uuid "23728889-0563-56f0-ac04-bd45fb3651f3" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188108 1074 "We want to divide $25$ by $6$.\n\nTo solve this division problem, we arrange $25$ objects in rows, placing $6$ objects in each row.\n\n- The number of full rows gives us the *whole number part* (also called the *quotient*).\n- If there is an incomplete row at the end, the number of objects in that row gives us the *remainder*. If there are no incomplete rows, the remainder equals $0$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5018af259bbdca6bbcd3fff3696f70895d0205efefb5fe11a3c8bf79f5baa86.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- $4$ full rows. So, the whole number part is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n- $1$ object in the incomplete row. So, the remainder is ${\\color{red}1}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = {\\color{blue}4} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{red}1}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188109 1069 "e-13430" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188109 1071 "![](images/e4/e4750a1a06e5b8abedb13242c75f9face11c93516b3aea4a62f91299c5a351f7.png)\n\nGiven the model above, find the quotient and remainder of $35 \\div 7$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188109 1072 #uuid "4d193f9d-5726-5a44-9f7f-f422eabfe982" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188109 1074 "We want to divide $35$ by $7$.\n\nTo solve this division problem, we arrange $35$ objects in rows, placing $7$ objects in each row.\n\n- The number of full rows gives us the *whole number part* (also called the *quotient*).\n- If there is an incomplete row at the end, the number of objects in that row gives us the *remainder*. If there are no incomplete rows, the remainder equals $0$.\n\n![](images/dd/dd7ccd809b46348292bb5bab40986f466a9a9276c454f567f959cf9abe8d32f1.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- $5$ full rows. So, the quotient is ${\\color{blue}5}$.\n- There are no incomplete rows. So, the remainder is ${\\color{red}0}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n35 \\div 7 = 5\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n35 \\div 7 = {\\color{blue}5} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{red}0}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188110 1069 "e-13431" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188110 1071 "![](images/f9/f94635b5c878f7da5e4c3df5e039178fd0bc95d0f04680d6ecd8b1f4c39a70a9.png)\n\nComplete the diagram above by arranging $17$ objects in rows with $5$ objects per row, and use this to find $17 \\div 5$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188110 1072 #uuid "d2161dbf-dc9c-5042-9293-8bf6d5c1dc2c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188110 1074 "We want to divide $17$ by $5$.\n\nTo solve this division problem, we arrange $17$ objects in rows, placing $5$ objects in each row.\n\n- The number of full rows gives us the *whole number part* (also called the *quotient*).\n- If there is an incomplete row at the end, the number of objects in that row gives us the *remainder*. If there are no incomplete rows, the remainder equals $0$.\n\n![](images/4b/4b1a18ee36793f79182e074e6ac75eae5b90127968f20fdff554c1196593b800.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- $3$ full rows. So, the quotient is ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n- $2$ object in the incomplete row. So, the remainder is ${\\color{red}2}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n17 \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}3} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{red}2}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188111 1069 "e-13433" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188111 1071 "Calculate the sum $5,326,143 + 271,612$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188111 1072 #uuid "a615493e-6c80-5f78-b28c-e018f2eca75d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188111 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & 2 & 6 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ + & 2 & 7 & 1 & 6 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & 2 & 6 & 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ + & 2 & 7 & 1 & 6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 5 & 9 & 7 & 7 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5,326,143 + 271,612 = 5,597,755\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188112 1069 "e-13434" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188112 1071 "What is $3,246,341+ 71,769$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188112 1072 #uuid "687c131d-77de-5e38-9996-348e410aeccb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188112 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}1; +\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ + & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3,246,341+ 71,769 = 3,318,110\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188113 1069 "e-13435" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188113 1071 "In $2020$, a certain country had an elderly population of $2,079,439$ and a non-elderly population of $8,019,826$. What was the total population of the country?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188113 1072 #uuid "1cb4579c-0e62-5950-8201-3fedf65037ff" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188113 1074 "To find the total population of the country, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n2,079,439 + 8,019,826\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 9 & 4 & 3 & 9 \\\\ + & 8 & 0 & 1 & 9 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 & 9 \\\\ + & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n2,079,439 + 8,019,826 = 10,099,265\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total population of the country was $10,099,265$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188114 1069 "e-13453" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188114 1071 "Using the number line below, round $4,335$ to the nearest hundred.\n\n![](images/32/32c0b79e187503960da2e08ae5b0485257e2c072196554c74bf323b9c1921ce0.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188114 1072 #uuid "84762a6f-1d1f-59c7-937e-3d811b890b95" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188114 1074 "Rounding a number to the nearest hundred means finding the closest multiple of one hundred to our number.\n\nThe closest multiples of $100$ to $4,335$ are $4,300$ and $4,400$.\n\nLet's add these multiples of $100$ to our diagram.\n\n![](images/9c/9c2337fee6b0dc02d448c9f836c6453b5b3255e5effbe255fce87dd130d8e1cd.png)\n\nSince $4,335$ is closer to ${\\color{DarkGreen}4,300}$ than ${\\color{SaddleBrown}4,400}$, this is the number we round to.\n\n![](images/08/0860c7cd62e3daacc21f47eb0a51279b24343d8c9435aa2b46d0bce29d19f528.png)\n\nTherefore, $4,335$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $4,300$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188115 1069 "e-13455" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188115 1071 "Round $25,234$ to the nearest ten." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188115 1072 #uuid "864edb29-97bd-5814-9aaa-6629486e5cf5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188115 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *tens* place:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}4}$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}4$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\nTherefore, $25,234$ rounded to the nearest ten is $25,230$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188116 1069 "e-13457" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188116 1071 "Round $200,238$ to the nearest hundred." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188116 1072 #uuid "f69848ff-0906-5084-aa61-2aa92e21cc5e" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188116 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *hundreds* place:\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *tens* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}3}$ | $8$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}3$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the hundreds (into tens and ones):\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\nTherefore, $200,238$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $200,200$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188117 1069 "e-13459" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188117 1071 "Round $7,452$ to the nearest thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188117 1072 #uuid "d879a6ee-9d5b-507a-ab09-32b29f349cab" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188117 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart. Notice the *thousands* place:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $4$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}4}$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}4$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones):\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\nTherefore, $7,452$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $7,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188118 1069 "e-13464" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188118 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$90 \\times 2 = 180$\nUse this to find the value of $92 \\times 2$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188118 1072 #uuid "17c8498a-ac73-56f0-b9c0-dacc65b39d4f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188118 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n90 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}{180}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $92 \\times 2$ using repeated addition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n91 \\cdot 2 &= 180 + 2 = 182 \\\\\n92 \\cdot 2 &= 182 + 2 = 184\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n92 \\times 2 = 184\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188119 1069 "e-13465" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188119 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$110 \\times 7 = 770$\nUse this to find the value of $113 \\times 7$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188119 1072 #uuid "7a2fe3bb-4e3f-5aa0-a984-181d007f7b38" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188119 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n110 \\times 7 = {\\color{blue}{770}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $113 \\times 7$ using repeated addition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n111 \\cdot 7 &= 770 + 7 = 777 \\\\\n112 \\cdot 7 &= 777 + 7 = 784 \\\\\n113 \\cdot 7 &= 784 + 7 = 791\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n113 \\times 7 = 791\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188120 1069 "e-13466" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188120 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n$1,200 \\times 5 = 6,000$\nUse this to find the value of $1,204 \\times 5$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188120 1072 #uuid "93694ee1-7aad-53d0-b06b-5c0d4db44a41" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188120 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n1,200 \\times 5 = {\\color{blue}{6,000}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $1,204 \\times 5$ using repeated addition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1, 201 \\cdot 5 &= 6, 000 + 5 = 6, 005 \\\\\n1, 202 \\cdot 5 &= 6, 005 + 5 = 6, 010 \\\\\n1, 203 \\cdot 5 &= 6, 010 + 5 = 6, 015 \\\\\n1, 204 \\cdot 5 &= 6, 015 + 5 = 6, 020\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,204 \\times 5 = 6,020\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188121 1069 "e-13471" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188121 1071 "Robert weighs $37$ pounds, and Milly weighs $41$ pounds. What is the combined weight of these two children?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188121 1072 #uuid "a3b8a8ec-f1f4-526e-8343-e871beeaf804" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188121 1074 "To find the combined weight, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n37+41\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 1\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}1} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 1; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}7}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n37 + 41 = 78\n$$\n\nSo, the combined weight of the children is $78$ pounds." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188122 1069 "e-13473" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188122 1071 "The regular time of a particular soccer game lasted $92$ minutes, and extra time lasted $33$ minutes. How long did the game last in total?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188122 1072 #uuid "f9784712-17d0-51c1-8e2a-5613c3e4eac0" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188122 1074 "To find the combined time, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n92 + 33\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 3\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 3; 5\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}9} + {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}2}$ below the *tens* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *hundreds*:\n\n$$\n1; 9\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1}+{\\color{blue}0}+{\\color{blue}0}={\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the game lasted $125$ minutes." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188123 1069 "e-13474" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188123 1071 "What is $58+55$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188123 1072 #uuid "2c0bb832-96f7-5e4d-9c21-ab8b057cc1b8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188123 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}3}$ below the *ones* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *tens*:\n\n$$\n1; 5\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 5; 3\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}5} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}1}$ below the *tens* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *hundreds*:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}8; + \\mid 5\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Notice that neither $58$ nor $55$ have a hundreds digit. Therefore, we place $\\color{red}0$ in their hundreds places:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 5\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nThen, adding the hundreds, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1}+{\\color{blue}0}+{\\color{blue}0}={\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n58 + 55 = 113\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188124 1069 "e-13475" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188124 1071 "Sandy bought $432$ grams of chocolate chips and used $302$ grams to make cookies. By rounding each value to the nearest hundred, estimate the weight of the remaining chocolate chips." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188124 1072 #uuid "2ef44202-0cfb-5c64-b4b6-6f50e1ef2665" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188124 1074 "To estimate the weight of the remaining chocolate chips, we need to approximate the difference\n\n$$\n432 - 302\n$$\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n\n$$\n432\\begin{vmatrix}- & 302 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 400 & -\\end{vmatrix}300\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference ${\\color{blue}{400}} - {\\color{blue}{300}}$:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ - & 3\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n432 - 302 \\approx 100\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sandy has approximately $100$ grams of chocolate chips remaining." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188125 1069 "e-13476" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188125 1071 "Estimate the difference $83,250 - 15,845$ by rounding each number to the nearest thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188125 1072 #uuid "620275ac-0c60-5313-bb8a-1c0c8e9bfb9a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188125 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest thousand:\n\n$$\n83, 250\\begin{vmatrix}- & 15, 845 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 83, 000 & -\\end{vmatrix}16, 000\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference ${\\color{blue}{83,000}} - {\\color{blue}{16,000}}$:\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}13 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, $83,250 - 15,845$ is approximately $67,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188126 1069 "e-13477" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188126 1071 "Estimate the difference $4,755,450 - 804,001$ by rounding each number to the nearest hundred thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188126 1072 #uuid "8c64e53c-b867-57a2-aece-92789884443c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188126 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest hundred thousand:\n\n$$\n4, 755, 450\\begin{vmatrix}- & 804, 001 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 4, 800, 000 & -\\end{vmatrix}800, 000\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference ${\\color{blue}{4,800,000}} - {\\color{blue}{800,000}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 8 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4,755,450 - 804,001$ is approximately $4,000,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188127 1069 "e-13478" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188127 1071 "What is $498 - 75$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188127 1072 #uuid "aa4088a0-8ac9-558d-b96e-dd1235f424e8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188127 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, and *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 & 8 \\\\ - & 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ - & 7\\end{vmatrix}5; 3\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}9} - {\\color{blue}7} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ - & 7\\end{vmatrix}5; 2 \\mid 3\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Subtracting the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} - {\\color{blue}0} = {\\color{blue}4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n498 - 75 = 423\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188128 1069 "e-13479" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188128 1071 "What is $335 - 61$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188128 1072 #uuid "b0a53464-6250-571c-9b4c-e79aba5d07a4" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188128 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 5 \\\\ - & 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}1} = {\\color{blue}4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ - & 6\\end{vmatrix}1; 4\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We cannot compute $3 - 6$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}13 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 1; 4\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Then, we subtract tens and hundreds:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}13 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n335 - 61 = 274\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188129 1069 "e-13480" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188129 1071 "![](images/79/79e9c0a72b513ef325ef19b03063b3d3a50a37dd2dad29ba42234ec991e17dee.png)\n\nThe fraction given below is equivalent to the fraction shown in the picture. What number is missing?\n$0$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188129 1072 #uuid "d4bb781e-a542-5292-9c02-5edb257f3c48" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188129 1074 "There are $10$ equal parts in the given shape in total. Of them, $7$ parts are shaded. So,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nof the shape is shaded.\n\nSince we are looking for a fraction of the form $0$ we now draw another shape that has the same shaded area but is divided into $100$ equal parts. To do that, we subdivide each part into $10$ equal pieces:\n\n![](images/19/19158f742efad61d8ed59f982865e9d4faf70502c46a828aa1bc1ec6e3e4bbe0.png)\n\nThe shape on the right is divided into\n\n$$\n10 \\times 10 = 100\n$$\n\nequal parts. Of them,\n\n$$\n7 \\times 10 = 70\n$$\n\nparts are shaded. So,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{70}{100}\n$$\n\nof the shape is shaded.\n\nHence, we obtain\n$70$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $\\color{blue}70$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188130 1069 "e-13481" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188130 1071 "What is $312 - 88$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188130 1072 #uuid "63836c3c-1c1a-5416-af21-7abec4a17393" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188130 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ - & 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $2 - 8$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place. Then, subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} - {\\color{blue}8} = {\\color{blue}4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 8; 4\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We cannot compute $0 - 8$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place.\nThen, we subtract tens and hundreds:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}2; -\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}8; 2 \\mid 2 \\mid 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n312 - 88 = 224\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188131 1069 "e-13482" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188131 1071 "What is $208 - 49$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188131 1072 #uuid "04516ea3-85d9-56d1-b1e5-f53a1db2d0b3" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188131 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 8 \\\\ - & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $8 - 9$, and there are $0$ tens to borrow from. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place first:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nThen, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place:\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 9\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then we subtract ones, tens, and hundreds:\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n208 - 49 = 159\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188132 1069 "e-13483" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188132 1071 "Find a decimal fraction with a denominator of $10$ that's equivalent to $\\dfrac{40}{100}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188132 1072 #uuid "81b1bb3a-21c9-54d8-961e-87ce4d1322b7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188132 1074 "To make a denominator of $10$, we divide the numerator and the denominator of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100}\n$$\n\nby $10$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{10}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188133 1069 "e-13484" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188133 1071 "Compute $\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac{27}{100}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188133 1072 #uuid "c3b8d65b-cf85-55ec-8bce-546c86bbe1fd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188133 1074 "To add the fractions, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $100$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 10}{10 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100} + \\frac{27}{100} = \\frac{30 + 27}{100} = \\frac{57}{100}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188134 1069 "e-13496" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188134 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{4}{25}$ as a decimal." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188134 1072 #uuid "e149c55c-e5e7-5f6e-baad-741a7af22dbe" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188134 1074 "First, we write this fraction as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator. To do that, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{25} &= \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{25 \\cdot 4} = \\frac{16}{100}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write this fraction as a decimal with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}16}{100} =0.{\\color{blue}16}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188135 1069 "e-13499" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188135 1071 "Which decimal number is equivalent to $\\dfrac{17}{200}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188135 1072 #uuid "a4d9add7-9140-5d8e-89b4-0b3e83668366" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188135 1074 "First, we write an equivalent fraction with $1,000$ as the denominator. To do that, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{17}{200} &= \\frac{17 \\cdot 5}{200 \\cdot 5} = \\frac{85}{1, 000}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write this fraction as a decimal with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{85}{1, 000} &= 0.085\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188136 1069 "e-13501" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188136 1071 "Using the fact that $5=\\dfrac{30}{6}$, express $5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{6}$ as an improper fraction." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188136 1072 #uuid "ad689cab-c8b8-5809-9650-fc39e6d55b0a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188136 1074 "Every mixed number can be written as the sum of its whole number and fractional parts. So, our mixed number can be written as\n\n$$\n5 \\, \\dfrac{1}{6} = 5 + \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{30}{6}}\n$$\n\nfor ${\\color{blue}5}$ and adding the fractions, we obtain\n\n$$\n5 + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{30}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{31}{6}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188137 1069 "e-13502" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188137 1071 "What improper fraction is equivalent to $5 \\,\\dfrac{3}{4}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188137 1072 #uuid "25cb7774-a597-5170-b5c8-6eecc1b361eb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188137 1074 "First, we write the whole number part $(5)$ as a fraction with $1$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n5 = \\dfrac{5}{1}\n$$\n\nWe put this over a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{1} = \\dfrac{5 \\times 4}{1 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{20}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n5\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{20}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{23}{4}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188138 1069 "e-13503" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188138 1071 "Lauren is listening to some songs while waiting for a train. The duration of the first song is $123$ seconds, the second is $182$ seconds, and the third is $157$ seconds. What is the total duration of all three songs?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188138 1072 #uuid "94a01b35-a5e9-53ce-adcc-0064c67b6e82" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188138 1074 "To find the total duration of all three tracks, we need to compute the sum\n\n$$\n123 + 182 + 157\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 5 \\mid 7\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers using the standard algorithm:\n$1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 2$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n123 + 182 + 157 = 462\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total duration of all three songs is $462$ seconds." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188139 1069 "e-13504" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188139 1071 "What is $31,252 + 12,892 + 25,034$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188139 1072 #uuid "d11072e3-684a-53ff-bbdd-7765d1daf4b7" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188139 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ + & 1 & 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers using the standard algorithm:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ + & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 1\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n31,252 + 12,892 + 25,034= 69,178\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188140 1069 "e-13505" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188140 1071 "$2,078,503 + 148,450 + 1,885,047 =$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188140 1072 #uuid "6a32a3eb-42c0-5e58-b205-3b1dd8d510e8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188140 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 8 & 5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ + & 1 & 4 & 8 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}0; +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 8 & 8 & 5 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}7\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers using the standard algorithm:\n\n$1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7 & 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 8 & 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,078,503 + 148,450 + 1,885,047 = 4,112,000\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188141 1069 "e-13515" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188141 1071 "Compute the value of $57,458 - 25,242$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188141 1072 #uuid "ca9a1b6a-8652-55b7-8594-9e15a556e1e5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188141 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 & 5 & 8 \\\\ - & 2 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 & 5 & 8 \\\\ - & 2 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n57,458 - 25,242 = 32,216\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188142 1069 "e-13517" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188142 1071 "What is $83,005 - 52,009$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188142 1072 #uuid "5f387e65-fd4c-546f-9019-d24450ca8284" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188142 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ - & 5 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left, borrowing where needed.\n\n$2\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 9 & 15 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 9 & 9\\end{vmatrix}6$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n83,005 - 52,009 = 30,996\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188143 1069 "e-13518" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188143 1071 "What is $23,861+5,026$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188143 1072 #uuid "7012ce0c-b065-5d0f-936d-36df46fe3e09" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188143 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ + & 5 & 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 8 & 6 & 1 \\\\ + & 5 & 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 8 & 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n23,861+5,026 = 28,887\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188144 1069 "e-13519" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188144 1071 "There are $32,355$ bees in a hive. On a particular day, $12, 247$ of the bees leave to find pollen. How many bees are left in the hive?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188144 1072 #uuid "424a75d4-02b2-53a9-8f68-01b9f7023d93" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188144 1074 "To determine how many bees are left in the hive, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n32, 355 - 12, 247\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ - & 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 7\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left, borrowing where needed.\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}15 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ - & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 0 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $20,108$ bees left in the hive." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188145 1069 "e-13522" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188145 1071 "Compute the value of $89,743+32,876$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188145 1072 #uuid "74d42742-c4af-5165-a6be-b04de77f95de" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188145 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ + & 3 & 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 9 & 7\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n89,723+32,876 = 122,619\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188146 1069 "e-13523" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188146 1071 "Mike wrote two essays. There are $11,391$ words in the first essay and $7,942$ words in the second essay. How many words did Mike write altogether?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188146 1072 #uuid "d04a7723-2732-5599-b091-a0f6663b7dbf" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188146 1074 "To find how many words Mike wrote altogether, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n11,391 + 7,942\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ + & 7 & 9 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ + & 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n11,391 + 7,942 = 19,333\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike wrote $19,333$ words in total." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188147 1069 "e-13524" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188147 1071 "There are $373$ boys and $415$ girls in a particular elementary school. What is the total number of students in the school?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188147 1072 #uuid "fbfdedec-d114-51bc-b838-09c0eb86eee3" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188147 1074 "To find the total number of students, we need to calculate the value of\n$373+415$.\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 5; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}1} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}8\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} + {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}7}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ + & 4\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n373 + 415 = 788\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are a total of $788$ students in the school." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188148 1069 "e-13525" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188148 1071 "Find the value of $84 - 31$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188148 1072 #uuid "673aeaa8-d99d-5195-af0a-d6e7ed05197d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188148 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 1\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} - {\\color{blue}1} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 1; 3\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}3} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n84 - 31 = 53\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188149 1069 "e-13526" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188149 1071 "What is $850 + 645$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188149 1072 #uuid "5a5a232e-d129-556e-afdb-660d475ae6f5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188149 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ + & 6\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}0} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ + & 6\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 5; 5\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} + {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}9}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ + & 6\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}4}$ below the *hundreds* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *thousands*:\n\n$$\n1; 8\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ + & 6\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 5\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Adding the thousands,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}0} + {\\color{blue}0} = {\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1; 0\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 0 \\\\ + & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 9 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n850 + 645 = 1,495\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188150 1069 "e-13527" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188150 1071 "Calculate $457 + 789$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188150 1072 #uuid "66a07950-c1e1-5e30-b590-4d42c4976d17" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188150 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ + & 7\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}6}$ below the *ones* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *tens*:\n\n$$\n1; 4\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ + & 7\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 9; 6\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}5} + {\\color{blue}8} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}4}$ below the *tens* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *hundreds*:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 8\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Adding the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}7} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nWe write ${\\color{blue}2}$ below the *hundreds* and carry ${\\color{red}1}$ to the *thousands*:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}7; +\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}9; 2 \\mid 4 \\mid 6\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Adding the thousands,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1}+{\\color{blue}0}+{\\color{blue}0}={\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n457 + 789 = 1,246\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188151 1069 "e-13528" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188151 1071 "Find the value of $72 - 56$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188151 1072 #uuid "0473f33e-0d4a-5f0c-bbd1-89a4003dc151" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188151 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $2 - 6$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place. Then we have\n\n$$\n7 - 1 = {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\ntens and $\\color{red}12$ ones:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 5 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} - {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}6}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 5 \\mid 6; 6\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6} - {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}1}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 5\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n72 - 56 = 16\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188152 1069 "e-13529" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188152 1071 "A college basketball team scored $25$ fewer points this month compared to last month. If they scored $82$ points last month, how many points did they score this month?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188152 1072 #uuid "81c825bd-5c13-597e-9683-ce3726b3200f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188152 1074 "To find the number of points the team scored this month, we need to find $82-25$.\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n$$\n8\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $2 - 5$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place. Then we have\n\n$$\n8 - 1 = {\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\ntens and $\\color{red}12$ ones:\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 2 \\mid 5\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} - {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}7}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 2 \\mid 5; 7\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}5}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}2; - \\mid 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the team scored $57$ points last month." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188153 1069 "e-13544" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188153 1071 "Sarah will use $90$ beads to make $3$ identical necklaces. How many beads will each necklace have?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188153 1072 #uuid "3d55ec05-3194-5453-932c-b7fee736d659" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188153 1074 "To determine the number of beads each necklace will have, we need to calculate $90\\div 3$.\n\nRemember that $90$ in expanded form is\n\n$$\n9 \\times 10\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 10 \\div 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 \\cdot 10 \\div 3 &=  \\\\\n9 \\div 3 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(9 \\div 3) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each necklace will have $30$ beads." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188154 1069 "e-13545" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188154 1071 "What is $280 \\div 7$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188154 1072 #uuid "715fe8f0-311e-5efe-8835-0059eb43ef3f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188154 1074 "Remember that $280$ is the same as $28$ tens. So, we have\n\n$$\n280 = 28 \\times {10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n280 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 28 \\cdot 10 \\div 7 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n28 \\cdot 10 \\div 7 &=  \\\\\n28 \\div 7 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n(28 \\div 7) \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n4 \\cdot 10 &=  \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n280\\div 7 = 40\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188155 1069 "e-13546" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188155 1071 "Compute the value of $3,200 \\div 4$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188155 1072 #uuid "da25ceb7-d74a-57be-a183-3bc0e0677871" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188155 1074 "Remember that $3,200$ is the same as $32$ hundreds. So, we have\n\n$$\n3,200 = 32 \\times {100}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3, 200 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 32 \\cdot 100 \\div 4 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap the order of the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n32 \\cdot 100 \\div 4 &=  \\\\\n32 \\div 4 \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n(32 \\div 4) \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n8 \\cdot 100 &=  \\\\\n&= 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3,200\\div 4 = 800\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188156 1069 "e-13552" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188156 1071 "Find the value of $3,500 \\div 10$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188156 1072 #uuid "9d450ca0-c6bb-59f8-8577-f076164aae9a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188156 1074 "Notice that the dividend $(3,500)$ is a whole number ending in a zero.\n\nTo divide a whole number ending in zero by $10$, we remove the final zero as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3, 500 \\div 10 &=  \\\\\n3, 500 &=  \\\\\n&= 350\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188157 1069 "e-13553" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188157 1071 "A secretary evenly distributes $1,600$ photocopies among $10$ folders. How many photocopies does each folder contain?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188157 1072 #uuid "01353514-8053-5d82-8d80-056b05525bf4" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188157 1074 "To determine the number of photocopies in each folder, we need to calculate the value of\n$1,600\\div 10$.\n\nNotice that the dividend $(1,600)$ is a whole number ending in a zero.\n\nTo divide a whole number ending in zero by $10$, we remove the final zero as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1, 600 \\div 10 &=  \\\\\n1, 600 &=  \\\\\n&= 160\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each folder contains $160$ photocopies." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188158 1069 "e-13555" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188158 1071 "Due to the rupture of a pipe that supplies water to a small town, $28,000$ liters of water were lost over a period of $100$ minutes. How much water was lost every minute?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188158 1072 #uuid "e256b286-ce37-5ee2-9240-f7068e609030" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188158 1074 "To determine how much water was lost each minute, we must calculate the value of $28,000 \\div 100$.\n\nNotice that the dividend $(28,000)$ is a whole number ending in *two* zeros.\n\nTo divide a whole number ending in two zeros by $100$, we remove the final two zeros as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n28, 000 \\div 100 &=  \\\\\n28, 000 &=  \\\\\n&= 280\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $280$ liters of water was lost every minute." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188159 1069 "e-13557" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188159 1071 "Find the value of $450,000 \\div 1,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188159 1072 #uuid "a8a9dacb-b74e-519a-8b71-5968251ec410" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188159 1074 "Notice that the dividend $(450,000)$ is a whole number ending in *three* zeros.\n\nTo divide a whole number ending in three zeros by $1,000$, we remove the final three zeros as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n450, 000 \\div 1, 000 &=  \\\\\n450, 000 &=  \\\\\n&= 450\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188160 1069 "e-13588" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188160 1071 "Round $311,695$ to the nearest ten." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188160 1072 #uuid "68a8d66d-55fd-5617-bf01-8f164d588331" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188160 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *tens* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $9$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}5$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place and increase the *tens* value by one:\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $6$ | $9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *tens* is the same as $1$ *hundred* and $0$ *tens*. So, we must write $0$ in the *tens* place and increase the number of *hundreds* by one:\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $311,695$ rounded to the nearest ten is $311,700$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188161 1069 "e-13590" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188161 1071 "Round $9,821$ to the nearest thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188161 1072 #uuid "b469fe24-a6a9-5c30-9244-90195749982a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188161 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *thousands* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}8}$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}8$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones) and increase the *thousands* value by one:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *thousands* is the same as $1$ *ten thousand* and $0$ *thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *thousands* place and increase the number of *ten-thousands* by one:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $9,821$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $10,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188162 1069 "e-13593" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188162 1071 "Round $2,997,523$ to the nearest ten thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188162 1072 #uuid "ac4ef306-73c7-585e-b166-76053eb1ffbc" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188162 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *ten-thousands* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *thousands* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}7}$ | $5$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}7$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow three steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in all places to the right of the ten-thousands (into thousands, hundreds, tens, and ones) and increase the *ten-thousands* value by one:\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $9$ | $9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *ten-thousands* is the same as $1$ *hundred thousand* and $0$ *ten-thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *ten-thousands* place and increase the number of *hundred-thousands* by one:\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\n- But, $9+1=10$ *hundred-thousands* is the same as $1$ *million* and $0$ *hundred-thousands*. So, we must write $0$ in the *hundred-thousands* place and increase the number of *millions* by one:\n\n| **millions** | **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $2,997,523$ rounded to the nearest ten thousand is $3,000,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188163 1069 "e-13606" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188163 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n\n$50\\times 6 = 300$\n\nUse this to find the value of $46\\times 6$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188163 1072 #uuid "1390ad31-2930-5410-9ea4-73e4c8d56fda" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188163 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n50\\times 6 = {\\color{blue}{300}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $46\\times 6$ using repeated subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n49 \\cdot 6 &= 300 - 6 = 294 \\\\\n48 \\cdot 6 &= 294 - 6 = 288 \\\\\n47 \\cdot 6 &= 288 - 6 = 282 \\\\\n46 \\cdot 6 &= 282 - 6 = 276\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n46\\times 6 = 276\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188164 1069 "e-13607" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188164 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n\n$110\\times 6 = 660$\n\nUse this to find the value of $107\\times 6$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188164 1072 #uuid "cf7a69ea-66ce-5cbc-aba1-8244156709bd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188164 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n110\\times 6 = {\\color{blue}{660}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $107\\times 6$ using repeated subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n109 \\cdot 6 &= 660 - 6 = 654 \\\\\n108 \\cdot 6 &= 654 - 6 = 648 \\\\\n107 \\cdot 6 &= 648 - 6 = 642\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n107\\times 6 = 642\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188165 1069 "e-13608" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188165 1071 "You're given the following multiplication problem:\n\n$1,200\\times 4 = 4,800$\n\nUse this to find the value of $1,196\\times 4$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188165 1072 #uuid "0538453b-19f4-51bd-b368-52eeddb8e41b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188165 1074 "We start with the given multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n1,200\\times 4 = {\\color{blue}{4,800}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the correct answer for $1,196\\times 4$ using repeated subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1, 199 \\cdot 4 &= 4, 800 - 4 = 4, 796 \\\\\n1, 198 \\cdot 4 &= 4, 796 - 4 = 4, 792 \\\\\n1, 197 \\cdot 4 &= 4, 792 - 4 = 4, 788 \\\\\n1, 196 \\cdot 4 &= 4, 788 - 4 = 4, 784\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,196\\times 4= 4,784\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188166 1069 "e-13610" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188166 1071 "Compute the value of $1,487,645 - 45,214$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188166 1072 #uuid "6aa66bd4-a393-55ba-ab77-9b66e84aa707" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188166 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 & 7 & 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}5; -\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 & 7 & 6 & 4 & 5 \\\\ - & 4 & 5 & 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 4 & 2 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1,487,645 - 45,214 = 1,442,431\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188167 1069 "e-13612" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188167 1071 "What is $2,354,702 - 1,446,254$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188167 1072 #uuid "f362fa55-18db-51e6-9a72-70722b6a0000" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188167 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 4 & 7 & 0 & 2 \\\\ - & 1 & 4 & 4 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 4$\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left, borrowing where needed.\n\n$1\\begin{vmatrix}13 & 4 & 14 & 6 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5 & 4 & 7 & 0\\end{vmatrix}2; -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 4 & 6 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}4; 9\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}8$\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,354,702 - 1,446,254 = 908,448\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188168 1069 "e-13614" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188168 1071 "Mike, a baseball player, earns $\\[math]1, 245,800$ per year, while Randy, a football player, makes $\\[/math]1,227,900$ per year. How much more does Mike earn per year compared to Randy?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188168 1072 #uuid "b53aefe0-e27f-5452-acf8-ff33cb9470fd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188168 1074 "To find out how much more Mike earns compared to Randy, we need to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n1, 245,800 - 1,227,900\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 5 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 1 & 2 & 2 & 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value from right to left, borrowing where needed.\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}14 & 18 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike earns $\\[math]17,900$ more per year." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188169 1069 "e-13620" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188169 1071 "![](images/19/19627a7f26233eb03f3df3df8620271931979106faa9820c17a7b3cb4ddf6b9f.png)\n\nThe area model above can be used to represent the following division problem:\n$00$\n\nWhat is the missing number?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188169 1072 #uuid "b68834de-4d01-53c0-a93e-3152455c7bfd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188169 1074 "Let's look at the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $300 + 50 + 4 = 354$\n- its width is $3$\n- its area is $900 + 150 + 12 = 1,062$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n1,062 = 354 \\times 3\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n1,062 \\div 3 = 354\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $354$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188170 1069 "e-13626" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188170 1071 "From left to right, find the missing numbers in the area model below.\n\n![](images/b9/b94bfa69e807cbdf01fd4fe49159f518ef4dbc378207dad134e6f29e877d7fdf.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188170 1072 #uuid "5879d02f-4f50-5728-a0d0-1c0f17c3233f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188170 1074 "We need to look at the blue and orange rectangles:\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $900$, and its width is $9$. Therefore, the length of this rectangle must be\n$900 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}100}$.\nLet's add this to our model.![](images/b7/b7304b7599468db827d860db27558753cdab56cf56d2b5f3730217d83bb72578.png)\n- Now, let's look at the orange rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $18$, and its width is $9$. Therefore, the length of this rectangle must be\n$18 \\div 9 = {\\color{blue}2}$.\nLet's add this to our model.![](images/ca/ca73ecf3de801e41f6a3db1b29a67166de8a5266d5ec85f1dab15e2825b5234e.png)\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the missing numbers are $100$ and $2$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188171 1069 "e-13629" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188171 1071 "![](images/50/5016331d80eea6a6b37f3c6fc7e05ba9ce80f6eca4a4bb361640a66c721a779b.png)\n\nA $1,971\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$ load of coal is equally distributed between $3$ hopper wagons. Using the area model above, find how much coal is loaded into each wagon." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188171 1072 #uuid "dcc53ea8-69b6-5e11-809a-b177518fd403" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188171 1074 "To find how much coal is loaded into each wagon, we need to calculate $1,971 \\div 3$.\n\nWe need to look at the orange and blue rectangles:\n\n- Let's first look at the blue rectangle (the one on the left-hand side). Its area is $1,800$, and its width is $3$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$1,800 \\div 3 = 600$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/00/000078b185ad61d89e64dd83a4f8830ab9b9604c111bf0047560a1774292acc1.png)\n- Now, let's look at the orange rectangle (the one on the right-hand side). Its area is $21$, and its width is $3$. Hence, the length of this rectangle must be\n$21 \\div 3 = 7$.\nLet's add this to our model:![](images/53/53d68ff5453e751d2059936624e986e69bde81437b577084f28d6eabfc716a10.png)\n\nNow, notice the following regarding the big rectangle:\n\n- its length is $600 + 50 + 7 = 657$\n- its width is $3$\n- its area is $1,800 + 150 + 21 = 1,971$\n\nTherefore, this area model can be used to represent the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n1,971 = 657 \\times 3\n$$\n\nWriting this as a division problem, we get\n\n$$\n1,971 \\div 3 = 657\n$$\n\nTherefore, each wagon is loaded with $657\\,\\textrm{ft}^3$ of coal." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188172 1069 "e-13640" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188172 1071 "What is $578 - 246$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188172 1072 #uuid "7edc3109-a2a4-5a9c-8294-2dd60cd58ef8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188172 1074 "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, and *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} - {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Subtracting the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n578 - 246 = 332\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188173 1069 "e-13641" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188173 1071 "What is $272 - 190$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188173 1072 #uuid "6d5a2406-6ab0-5e40-95e5-0ea3df46681e" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188173 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} - {\\color{blue}0} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 0; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We cannot compute $7 - 9$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}17 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 9 \\mid 0; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Then, we subtract tens and hundreds:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}17 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n272 - 190 = 82\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188174 1069 "e-13644" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188174 1071 "What is $332 - 174$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188174 1072 #uuid "22538f51-5088-552f-9499-61c9e704a29a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188174 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 4\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $2 - 4$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place.\nThen, subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} - {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}8}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 7 \\mid 4; 8\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We cannot compute $2 - 7$. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place.\nThen, we subtract tens and hundreds:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}4; 1 \\mid 5 \\mid 8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n332 - 174 = 158\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188175 1069 "e-13646" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188175 1071 "What is $208 - 169$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188175 1072 #uuid "e22675fe-efae-5a21-8b2b-234fb062fe95" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188175 1074 "First, we line up our numbers:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** We cannot compute $8 - 9$, and there are $0$ tens to borrow from. So, we borrow $1$ from the *hundreds* place first:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 6 \\mid 9\n$$\n\nThen, we borrow $1$ from the *tens* place:\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 6 \\mid 9\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we subtract ones, tens, and hundreds:\n\n$$\n9\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n208 - 169 = 39\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188176 1069 "e-13655" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188176 1071 "What decimal is shown as the plotted point on the number line below?\n\n![](images/0a/0acfadc46b04204bf67dc4569db9b95f709ab612bb783f1e9749cab567f234c1.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188176 1072 #uuid "f6354f6e-c23a-5762-baf2-e401d8a89b05" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188176 1074 "Each line segment between two whole numbers is split into $10$ equal parts. So, each part gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} = 0.1\n$$\n\n(one-tenth) of a whole.\n\nNow, notice that our point is $\\color{blue}5$ steps to the right of ${\\color{red}8}$.\n\n![](images/b3/b34d89736fd7836cb03a49612a7cdc8a0cd052eb1a7482b334a11bf21e07dcbc.png)\n\nThe decimal has $\\color{red}8$ ones and $\\color{blue}5$ tenths.\nTherefore, the point represents the decimal ${\\color{red}8}.\\!{\\color{blue}5}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188177 1069 "e-13659" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188177 1071 "What decimal is shown as the plotted point on the number line below?\n\n![](images/b7/b7f7a4cd2fc9342f38b42ee156dd96245139593689c1b66e705bb76d6385f8df.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188177 1072 #uuid "0bc75112-e185-5b1f-be0a-16f27763f584" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188177 1074 "The line segment between each tenth is split into $10$ equal parts. So, each part gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{100} = 0.01\n$$\n\n(one-hundredth) of a whole.\n\nNotice that our point is $\\color{blue}4$ steps to the right of ${\\color{red}0.0}$.\n\n![](images/5c/5c8c4f53aa4d445182c3269ba4be61ddf62c6721a3aea009ff7f9629f9e793e8.png)\n\nHence, the decimal has ${\\color{red}0}$ ones, ${\\color{red}0}$ tenths, and ${\\color{blue}4}$ hundredths. Therefore, the point represents the decimal $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}{\\color{red}0.0}{\\color{blue}4}}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188178 1069 "e-13664" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188178 1071 "What mixed number is shown in the picture below?\n\n![](images/34/34fd453992b6da6577309a9128280c8c4003ecfa82b0db4c9418688bca35caf8.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188178 1072 #uuid "e67fde69-f522-544a-9d50-601c28734b7d" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188178 1074 "There are $\\color{red}2$ shapes completely shaded:\n\n![](images/97/97da143100740dc953cd0cd802a265feabd91c99fba78d8d27d785ccd9931993.png)\n\nSo, there are $\\color{red}2$ wholes.\n\nAdditionally, one shape is partially shaded:\n\n![](images/41/41d85faa9e0930416ea014edd20f0ae8b4ebc375bcc1c307db951c7277277ab3.png)\n\nThis shape has $\\color{blue}3$ parts, and $\\color{blue}2$ parts are shaded. So,\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nof this shape is shaded.\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}\\, {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188179 1069 "e-13666" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188179 1071 "Draw a picture that represents the mixed number $2\\dfrac{3}{7}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188179 1072 #uuid "ef2782c2-1e4a-561e-9fa0-0a4c45281c28" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188179 1074 "The mixed number\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{blue}7}\n$$\n\nmeans we must have\n\n- $\\color{red}2$ entirely shaded shapes, and\n- an additional shape that represents $\\color{blue}\\dfrac37$.\n\nTherefore, we could represent\n\n$$\n2\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nusing the following picture:\n\n![](images/76/76937bdf5679e1b206beffa53f7447cb8b84c68169092a09a090b9b8ea6b91ed.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188180 1069 "e-13711" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188180 1071 "Plot the number $4.3$ on the number line below.\n\n![](images/65/65c5187ef89e7d057893908eb92ea8c0a9b7724e1b1f8c30696e532a847988f2.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188180 1072 #uuid "6c120263-d7d1-5b79-acd7-807f7a1efcfa" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188180 1074 "Each of the line segments between the whole numbers $4$ and $5$ is split into $10$ equal parts. So, each part gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} = 0.1\n$$\n\n(one-tenth) of a whole.\n\nThe decimal ${\\color{red}4}.\\!{\\color{blue}3}$ has ${\\color{red}4}$ ones and ${\\color{blue}3}$ tenths.\nSo, we start at the point $\\color{red}4$ and move $\\color{blue}3$ steps to the right:\n\n![](images/3c/3c49c71a1fc7d63a117e5794741509b814fcc1882e14ad075b60640dcd3c8c3b.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188181 1069 "e-13712" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188181 1071 "Plot the number $3.04$ on the number line below.\n\n![](images/e9/e98ec17f42509f7607663caa0634bd468fd713d9d25a110484c8705037ab9880.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188181 1072 #uuid "d7b90202-4882-5574-a258-cf674d4fe004" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188181 1074 "Each of the line segments between the tenths $3.0$ and $3.1$ is split into $10$ equal parts. So, each part gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{100} = 0.01\n$$\n\n(one-hundredth) of a whole.\n\nThe decimal ${\\color{red}3.0}{\\color{blue}4}$ has ${\\color{red}3}$ ones, ${\\color{red}0}$ tenths, and ${\\color{blue}4}$ hundredths.\nSo, we start at the point $\\color{red}3.0$ and move $\\color{blue}4$ steps to the right:\n\n![](images/53/53dfc65854dc091d05e7a30c80c6c5658ac9147bfca06d38cc759ee1733ba863.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188182 1069 "e-13861" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188182 1071 "Using the number line below, round $7,386$ to the nearest hundred.\n\n![](images/b9/b902e581f50e8d8d3b452a51d83742fcd504eec237ed5c87fa493169ae7a7a45.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188182 1072 #uuid "719b91ea-5bae-5850-b60d-099c3f6c4623" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188182 1074 "Rounding a number to the nearest hundred means finding the closest multiple of one hundred to our number.\n\nThe closest multiples of $100$ to $7,386$ are $7,300$ and $7,400$.\n\nLet's add these multiples of $100$ to our diagram.\n\n![](images/6a/6a9f6116595f915c299c7fab3b5a499a8ed86cb642e3f2b42390ef5e70d7c6ea.png)\n\nSince $7,386$ is closer to ${\\color{DarkGreen}7,400}$ than ${\\color{SaddleBrown}7,300}$, this is the number we round to.\n\n![](images/ac/acbbac8b2bcc44bb48368e0f6b9517a909f9d5237eb22e494e6b1a903cb451d5.png)\n\nTherefore, $7,386$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $7,400$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188183 1069 "e-13867" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188183 1071 "Round $54,385$ to the nearest ten." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188183 1072 #uuid "3bda4a9b-66c0-55d1-be09-3ec151892ba2" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188183 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *tens* place:\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}5}$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}5$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ into the ones place:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\n- Then, we increase the tens value by one:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9}$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $54,385$ rounded to the nearest ten is $54,390$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188184 1069 "e-13868" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188184 1071 "Round $7,825$ to the nearest thousand." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188184 1072 #uuid "7b54c962-a840-5a34-8cd9-d18f5081194a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188184 1074 "We start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *thousands* place.\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *hundreds* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}8}$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nSince $\\color{red}8$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ into all places to the right of the thousands (into hundreds, tens, and ones):\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ |\n\n- Then, we increase the *thousands* value by one:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, $7,825$ rounded to the nearest thousand is $8,000$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188185 1069 "e-14051" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188185 1071 "What is $0.2 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ expressed in $\\textrm g$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188185 1072 #uuid "a8a6ddf1-4401-5f68-9eba-e6d1162d33eb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188185 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2kg &= 0.2kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.2kg \\cdot \\frac{1000g}{kg} \\\\\n&= 0.2 \\cdot 1000g \\\\\n&= 200g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $200 \\,\\text{g}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188186 1069 "e-14052" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188186 1071 "What is $2.25 \\,\\textrm{g}$ expressed in $\\textrm{mg}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188186 1072 #uuid "f565f22a-58de-5553-b618-d55f088be079" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188186 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $2.25 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2.25g &= 2.25g \\cdot \\frac{1000mg}{g} \\\\\n&= 2.25g \\cdot \\frac{1000mg}{g} \\\\\n&= 2.25 \\cdot 1000mg \\\\\n&= 2250mg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.25 \\,\\textrm{g}$ is equivalent to $2\\,250\\,\\text{mg}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188187 1069 "e-14053" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188187 1071 "What is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ expressed in $\\textrm{m}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188187 1072 #uuid "a9c05106-42eb-5b13-9337-ba5ff7a39f8b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188187 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15km &= 15km \\cdot \\frac{1000m}{km} \\\\\n&= 15km \\cdot \\frac{1000m}{km} \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 1000m \\\\\n&= 15000m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $15\\,000\\,\\textrm m$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188188 1069 "e-14057" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188188 1071 "What is $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ expressed in $\\textrm{kg}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188188 1072 #uuid "8e2f9e3e-e0b3-53d5-88e6-38cbcb37af59" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188188 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm g)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{kg})$, we need to multiply $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{1000\\,\\textrm g}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1300g &= 1300g \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000g} \\\\\n&= 1300g \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000g} \\\\\n&= 1300 \\cdot \\frac{kg}{1000} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1300}{1000}kg \\\\\n&= 1.3kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,300 \\,\\textrm{g}$ is equivalent to $1.3 \\,\\text{kg}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188189 1069 "e-14058" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188189 1071 "What is $4.5\\,\\textrm{cm}$ expressed in $\\textrm{mm}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188189 1072 #uuid "3b0be6b5-3a38-5494-a889-9db0fcdc64ca" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188189 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nThere are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $4.5\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.5cm &= 4.5cm \\cdot \\frac{10mm}{cm} \\\\\n&= 4.5cm \\cdot \\frac{10mm}{cm} \\\\\n&= 4.5 \\cdot 10mm \\\\\n&= 45mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4.5\\,\\textrm{cm}$ is equivalent to $45 \\,\\textrm{mm}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188190 1069 "e-14060" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188190 1071 "What is $0.5 \\,\\textrm{h}$ expressed in $\\textrm{min}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188190 1072 #uuid "dc6e1754-6a67-56d6-9693-4bf78835a1dd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188190 1074 "There are $60\\,\\textrm{min}$ per $\\,\\textrm{h}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.5\\,\\textrm{h}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.5h &= 0.5h \\cdot \\frac{60min}{h} \\\\\n&= 0.5h \\cdot \\frac{60min}{h} \\\\\n&= 0.5 \\cdot 60min \\\\\n&= 30min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.5\\,\\textrm{h}$ is equivalent to $30\\,\\textrm{min}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188191 1069 "e-14061" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188191 1071 "What is $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ expressed in $\\textrm{km}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188191 1072 #uuid "f2428635-7ffa-50c9-b629-fb49edc6cd88" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188191 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{m})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{km})$, we need to multiply $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1800m &= 1800m \\cdot \\frac{km}{1000m} \\\\\n&= 1800m \\cdot \\frac{km}{1000m} \\\\\n&= 1800 \\cdot \\frac{km}{1000} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1800}{1000}km \\\\\n&= 1.8km\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,800\\,\\textrm{m}$ is equivalent to $1.8\\,\\textrm{km}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188192 1069 "e-14063" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188192 1071 "What is $15 \\, \\textrm{min}$ expressed in $\\textrm{s}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188192 1072 #uuid "ae4d65ef-c783-5445-bdbe-075a2c099b19" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188192 1074 "There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $15\\,\\textrm{min}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15min &= 15min \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= 15min \\cdot \\frac{60s}{min} \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 60s \\\\\n&= 900s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15\\,\\textrm{min}$ is equivalent to $900\\,\\textrm{s}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188193 1069 "e-14064" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188193 1071 "What is $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ expressed in $\\textrm{m}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188193 1072 #uuid "18e8703f-dc2c-54f7-8135-986cdd61760f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188193 1074 "We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nThere are $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{cm})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{m})$, we need to multiply $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4000cm &= 4000cm \\cdot \\frac{m}{100cm} \\\\\n&= 4000cm \\cdot \\frac{m}{100cm} \\\\\n&= 4000 \\cdot \\frac{m}{100} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4000}{100}m \\\\\n&= 40m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ is equivalent to $40\\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188194 1069 "e-14065" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188194 1071 "What is $3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}$ expressed in $\\textrm{min}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188194 1072 #uuid "93b8507f-f278-519b-83c8-933a2e527aea" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188194 1074 "There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of time $(\\textrm{s})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{min})$, we need to multiply $3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{60\\,\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3600s &= 3600s \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s} \\\\\n&= 3600s \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s} \\\\\n&= 3600 \\cdot \\frac{min}{60} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3600}{60}min \\\\\n&= 60min\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,600\\,\\textrm{s}$ is equivalent to $60\\,\\textrm{min}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188195 1069 "e-15112" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188195 1071 "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n = 30$ conducted without replacement from a population of $N=100$ individuals with population mean $\\mu = 2$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma = 4$.\n\nIf $\\Phi(z)$ denotes the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, find the probability that the sample mean is between $2.1$ and $2.3$. Express your answer in terms of $\\Phi(z)$, the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188195 1072 #uuid "f023957c-f76e-5a68-931d-4133642c73f5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188195 1074 "Suppose that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe typically use this approximation when\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\nHowever, if the population size is not large compared to the sample size, the sample data is not independent. To account for this, we add a correction factor to the variance of the sample mean distribution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the population size.\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis called the finite population correction factor, and it is appropriate to use it when $n > 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample includes more than $5\\%$ of the entire population).\n\nNote that if a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 30, \\qquad \\mu = 2, \\qquad \\sigma = 4\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nn > 5\\%\\cdot N = 0.05\\cdot 100= 5\n$$\n\nwe must use the following finite population correction factor:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} = \\dfrac{100-30}{100-1} = \\dfrac{70}{99}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(2, \\frac{4^{2}}{30} \\cdot \\frac{70}{99}); ∼N(2, \\frac{112}{297})\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.1 < \\overline{X} < 2.3)$. So, we convert $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2.1 < X^(―) < 2.3) &= P(\\frac{2.1 - 2}{\\sqrt{\\frac{112}{297}}} < Z < \\frac{2.3 - 2}{\\sqrt{\\frac{112}{297}}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(0.16 < Z < 0.49) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.49) - P(Z \\le 0.16) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.49) - Φ(0.16)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(2.1 < \\overline{X} < 2.3) \\approx \\Phi(0.49) - \\Phi(0.16)\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188196 1069 "e-15113" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188196 1071 "A study aims to assess the average scores of a population of $N=676$ students in a particular school district. They took the scores of a sample of $n=52$ students. Given that the population mean score is $\\mu=75\\%$ with population standard deviation $\\sigma=15$, what is the probability that the mean score of the students in the sample is more than $70\\%$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -2.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0062 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0060 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0059 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0057 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -2.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0080 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0078 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0075 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -2.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0107 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0104 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0102 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0099 \\,$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188196 1072 #uuid "708b5b45-4038-5b6a-89bd-87ea4445c8c3" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188196 1074 "Suppose that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe typically use this approximation when\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\nHowever, if the population size is not large compared to the sample size, the sample data is not independent. To account for this, we add a correction factor to the variance of the sample mean distribution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\cdot \\frac{N-n}{N-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the population size.\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis called the finite population correction factor, and it is appropriate to use it when $n > 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample includes more than $5\\%$ of the entire population).\n\nNote that if a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 52, \\qquad \\mu = 75, \\qquad \\sigma = 15\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nn > 5\\%\\cdot N = 0.05\\cdot 676= 33.8\n$$\n\nwe must use the following finite population correction factor:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} = \\dfrac{676-52}{676-1} = \\dfrac{208}{225}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(75, \\frac{15^{2}}{52} \\cdot \\frac{208}{225}); ∼N(75, 4)\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overline{X} \\gt 70)$. So, we convert $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^(―) > 70) &= P(Z > \\frac{70 - 75}{\\sqrt{4}}) \\\\\n&= P(Z > - 2.5) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le - 2.5) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-2.5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.5) = 0.0062\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^(―) > 70) &= 1 - Φ(-2.5) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.0062 \\\\\n&= 0.9938\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188197 1069 "e-15117" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188197 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=65$ from a population of size $N=310$ where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.8$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n![](images/39/391592a451e201eb026c1df951319c7ba6915d9650a6b31e8d7e7ef52d932e1a.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188197 1072 #uuid "aef964da-920b-5cd0-99ae-e673811e6250" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188197 1074 "Since $n=65$, $N=310$, and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.8\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 65 \\cdot 0.8 = 52 > 5$,\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 65 \\cdot (1-0.8) = 13 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use a normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size.\n\nNotice that the finite population correction factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis necessary when the sample size is a significant proportion of the population, typically exceeding $5\\%\\cdot N$. In our case, we have\n\n$$\n65 > 5\\%\\cdot N = 5\\%\\cdot 310 = 15.5\n$$\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.645) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is\n\n$$\n0.8 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.8(1 - 0.8))/(65) \\cdot \\frac{310 - 65}{310 - 1}}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n$0.8 ± 0.073$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188198 1069 "e-15118" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188198 1071 "A total of $N=400$ students use an e-learning platform. In a random sample of $n=130$ of these students, $78$ declared they were satisfied with the quality of the course materials.\n\nFind a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of students satisfied with the course materials.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188198 1072 #uuid "2ad6bf34-d2ea-5bfc-bd2d-bce53deb8328" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188198 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all students subscribed to the e-learning platform,\n- some of the students are satisfied with the quality of the course materials, some aren't,\n- the size of the random sample is $n=130$,\n- the size of the population is $N=400$, and\n- the proportion of students from the sample who are satisfied can be found as follows:\n$\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{78}{130} = 0.6$\n\nNow, we have that $n=130$, $N=400$, and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.6\n$$\n\nso\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 130\\cdot 0.6= 78> 5$,\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 130\\cdot (1-0.6) = 52> 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use a normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size.\n\nNotice that the finite population correction factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis necessary when the sample size is a significant proportion of the population, typically exceeding $5\\%\\cdot N$. In our case, we have\n\n$$\n130> 5\\%\\cdot N = 5\\%\\cdot 400 = 20\n$$\n\nThen, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/95/95a498945e3bc43a2e40ebc2d31206c4b49afba182bf0453a72e1e7201d0a8b4.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.05}=1.645{:}\n$$\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is\n\n$$\n0.6 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.6(1 - 0.6))/(130) \\cdot \\frac{400 - 130}{400 - 1}}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n0.6 ± 0.058\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n(0.6- 0.058, \\, 0.6+ 0.058) = (0.54, 0.66)\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188199 1069 "e-15151" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188199 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n$\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p,\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\right)$,\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\n1. A sample of $120$ apricots from a population of $20\\,000$, where $75\\%$ of the apricots weigh more than $50$ g.\n2. A sample of $25$ gym members from a population containing $200$ members, where $80\\%$ of the gym's members work out for more than $100$ minutes each visit.\n3. A sample of $45$ flights in an airport, given that $90\\%$ of flights arrive on time. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188199 1072 #uuid "ddfa9789-7019-5939-b675-bcef59e5ef4c" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188199 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n\\leq 5\\% \\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n$n|= 120; \\le 5% \\cdot N; = 0.05 \\cdot 20000; = 1000✓; np|= 120(0.75); = 90; > 5✓; n(1 - p)|= 120(1 - 0.75); = 30; > 5.✓$\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n- In situation II, we have\n$n|= 25,; 5% \\cdot N|= 0.05 \\cdot 200; = 10; < 25.\\cdot$\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n- In situation III, we're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied. For the other conditions, we have\n$np|= 45(0.9); = 40.5; > 5✓; n(1 - p)|= 45(1 - 0.9); = 4.5; ≯5.\\cdot$\nSince $n(1-p) \\ngtr 5$, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188200 1069 "e-15152" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188200 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9292 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9306 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9319 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9418 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9429 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9441 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9525 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9535 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9545 \\,$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188200 1072 #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188200 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5$, and $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 84$, and\n- the population proportion is $p = 0.42$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 84(0.42) = 35.28 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 84(1-0.42) = 48.72 > 5\n$$\n\nand $n=84$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\np^(^) \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n)); ∼N(0.42, (0.42(1 - 0.42))/(84)); ∼N(0.42, 0.0029)\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p} > 0.5)$.\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(p^(^) > 0.5) &= P(Z > \\frac{0.5 - 0.42}{\\sqrt{0.0029}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z > 1.49) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 1.49) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(1.49)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(1.49) &= 0.9319\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(p^(^) > 0.5) &= 1 - Φ(1.49) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9319 \\\\\n&= 0.0681\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188201 1069 "e-15153" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188201 1071 "A popular streaming service has a database of $100$ million songs, of which $64$ million have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. If a user randomly creates a playlist with $128$ songs, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $64$ and $96$ (inclusive) songs in the sample have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, -3.4 \\,$ $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ $\\, -3.3 \\,$ $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ $\\, -3.2 \\,$ $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ $\\, 0.0006 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.07 \\,$$\\, 0.08 \\,$$\\, 0.09 \\,$$\\, 2.5 \\,$ $\\, 0.9949 \\,$ $\\, 0.9951 \\,$ $\\, 0.9952 \\,$ $\\, 2.6 \\,$ $\\, 0.9962 \\,$ $\\, 0.9963 \\,$ $\\, 0.9964 \\,$ $\\, 2.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.9972 \\,$ $\\, 0.9973 \\,$ $\\, 0.9974 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188201 1072 #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188201 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5$, and $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 128$, and\n- the population proportion is $p = \\dfrac{64\\,000\\,000}{100\\,000\\,000} = 0.64$.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 128(0.64) = 81.92 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 128(1-0.64) = 46.08 > 5\n$$\n\nand the $n=128$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\np^(^) \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n)); ∼N(0.64, (0.64(1 - 0.64))/(128)); ∼N(0.64, 0.0018)\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $64$ and $96$ of the songs in the playlist last less than $3$ minutes, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{64}{128} \\le p^(^) & \\le \\frac{96}{128} \\\\ 0.5 \\le p^(^) & \\le 0.75\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP(0.5 \\leq \\widehat{\\,p} \\leq 0.75)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.5 \\le p^(^) \\le 0.75) &= P(\\frac{0.5 - 0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{0.75 - 0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(-3.30 \\le Z \\le 2.59) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 2.59) - P(Z \\le - 3.30) \\\\\n&= Φ(2.59) - Φ(-3.30)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.59) = 0.9952, Φ(-3.30) = 0.0005\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.5 \\le p^(^) \\le 0.75) &= Φ(2.59) - Φ(-3.30) \\\\\n&= 0.9952 - 0.0005 \\\\\n&= 0.9947\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188202 1069 "e-1592" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188202 1071 "Complete the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ The number $47, 683$ has $4$ $\\underline{\\phantom{0000000000000}}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188202 1072 #uuid "5dc97181-d3cc-5bb0-a8e4-907c35925526" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188202 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $7$ | $6$ | $8$ | $3$ |\n\nWe have $4$ in the ten-thousands place. So the complete statement is as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number $47, 683$ has $4$ ten-thousands.*" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188203 1069 "e-16378" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188203 1071 "Given that $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Po}(12)$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,60$ are independent Poisson random variables, what is the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188203 1072 #uuid "04ad6c63-8642-51e7-bf95-70d8b97f4f9b" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188203 1074 "Recall that the central limit theorem states the following:\n\n> *Suppose that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as*\n> $\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)$.\n\nThe expected value and the variance of a Poisson random variable $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\lambda\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 60, \\\\\nμ &= E[X_{i}] = 12, \\\\\nσ^{2} &= Var[X_{i}] = 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the approximate distribution of the sample mean $\\overline{X}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nX^(―) \\mid ∼N(μ, \\frac{σ^{2}}{n}); ∼N(12, \\frac{12}{60}); ∼N(12, 1 5)\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188204 1069 "e-1810" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188204 1071 "$35\\div 6$ lies between which two whole numbers?\n\n1. $4$ and $5$\n2. $5$ and $6$\n3. $6$ and $7$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188204 1072 #uuid "422fd75d-d971-5fe9-93e8-2abaf4a4daa6" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188204 1074 "We try all of the given pairs, starting with the smallest:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n4\\times6 = 24\n$$\n\nwhich is smaller than $35$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{5}}\\times6 = 30\n$$\n\nwhich is smaller than $35$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{6}}\\times6 = 36\n$$\n\nwhich is bigger than $35$\n\nTherefore, $35\\div 6$ lies between ${\\color{blue}{5}}$ and ${\\color{red}{6}}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188205 1069 "e-1811" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188205 1071 "Express $16\\div 3$ as a mixed number." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188205 1072 #uuid "a2c5b3eb-25b5-5723-9f59-8ec21de38dbb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188205 1074 "We start by writing down the different multiples of $3$, stopping when we get to one that's bigger than $16$:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n2 \\times 3 = 6\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n3\\times 3 = 9\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n4\\times 3 = 12\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{5}} \\times 3 = {\\color{purple}{15}}\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n6\\times 3 =18\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n(too big)\n\nTherefore $16\\div 3$ has a whole number part of ${\\color{blue}{5}}$.\n\nWe now find the remainder:\n\n$$\n16 - {\\color{purple}{15}} = \\color{red}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n16\\div 3 = {\\color{blue}{5}}\\,\\textrm{R}{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the remainder as a fraction by dividing it by the original divisor of $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{1}}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n16\\div 3 = {\\color{blue}{5}}\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}{1}}{3}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1069 "e-3144" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1071 "Express $\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1$ in terms of $\\sec x$ only." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1072 #uuid "b0e16600-3365-562f-8cc8-bf25e0c1638a" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188206 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} x = \\sec^{2} x \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} x = \\sec^{2} x - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{2} x = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} x\\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{4} x + 1 &= \\tan^{2} x(\\sec^{2} x - \\tan^{2} x) + 1 \\\\\n&= \\tan^{2} x \\cdot (1) + 1 \\\\\n&= \\tan^{2} x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} x + 1 &= \\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1069 "e-3455" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1072 #uuid "91980d9e-0e4f-546e-b9ee-3117c4950aed" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1069 "e-3463" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1072 #uuid "915ff3d9-c631-5134-82ff-58dbf0167602" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ}{\\sec θ + \\tan θ} &= ((\\sec θ - \\tan θ)(\\sec θ + \\tan θ))/(\\sec θ + \\tan θ) \\\\\n&= ((\\sec θ - \\tan θ)(\\sec θ + \\tan θ))/((\\sec θ + \\tan θ)) \\\\\n&= \\sec θ - \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} = \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1069 "e-524" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1071 "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, in this process." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1072 #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188209 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to its *radius.* In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kr$\nwhere $k > 0$.\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by\n$A=\\pi r^2$.\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(πr^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= 2πr\\frac{dr}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dA}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n2πr\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -kr \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{kr}{2πr} \\\\\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= -\\frac{k}{2π}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK = \\dfrac{k}{2\\pi} > 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}t} = -K\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1069 "e-5445" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$. If $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=1$, then what is $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$?" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1072 #uuid "4b196d96-a95f-53d6-a0c5-7233de14a401" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188210 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given transformation, we have $n=3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(T) + nullity(T) &= n \\\\\nrank(T) + 1 &= 3 \\\\\nrank(T) &= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1069 "e-631" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1072 #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188211 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} = (\\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dθ}))\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta$.\n\nUsing the fact that $r=5$, we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -5\\sin θ, \\\\\ny^{′}(θ) &= 5\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{5\\cos θ}{-5\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dθ}(-\\cot θ))/(-5\\sin θ) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\csc^{2} θ}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\csc θ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{3} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188212 1069 "e-660" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188212 1071 "Given that $a_n = 3^{n}$ for $n\\geq 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$\n2. $a_n$ is an increasing sequence\n3. $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188212 1072 #uuid "b959d11d-b122-5fd8-b008-e702fb33f328" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188212 1074 "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, $a_n = 3^{n}$ is positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$\\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}}|= \\frac{3^{n + 1}}{3^{n}}; = 3^{n + 1 - n}; = 3^{1}; = 3; \\ge 1$.\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188213 1069 "e-7251" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188213 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and let $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4$. Consider the following statement:\n\n> *If $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\textrm{__________}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n2. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n3. $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188213 1072 #uuid "0eddf8b0-bdd9-55db-9388-d39bd91c24c1" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188213 1074 "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4$, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $Im(T) = R^{4}; rank(T) = 4; Ker(T) = {0}; nullity(T) = 0$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3$.\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n$nullity(T)|= 4 - rank(T); = 4 - 3; = 1$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188214 1071 "What mixed number is shown on the number line?\n\n![](images/22/22d1b303ecbd7efe2f623e78e8ac95c8c6d32e5fbedef3085787002dd850112f.png)" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188214 1072 #uuid "603520e6-f746-5b18-9533-939414182095" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188214 1074 "The line segment from $0$ to $1$ is split into $\\color{blue}6$ equal parts. Each part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nof a whole.\n\n![](images/9a/9abf61dfad134585ffbd0f094a3b854904c8a46c007065df80710baa1eb27c86.png)\n\nStarting from zero, we land on our point by making $\\color{red}2$ whole steps plus $\\color{blue}1$ part step:\n\n![](images/b5/b5d8f4f0a4c001f08fad1369cc77550d15d7421bab7c2a928cd964eebf2389a3.png)\n\nTherefore, the mixed number is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{1}{6}}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188215 1069 "e-9187" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188215 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n${\\frac{2}{9}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188215 1072 #uuid "e4007ddd-a5ed-5f3d-96cc-4c911d97a615" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188215 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}x \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}xy^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in [0, 3] \\times [0, 2]$. Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{18}xy^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188216 1069 "e-9188" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188216 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n${\\frac{10}{3x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(X \\geq 3, Y \\leq 2)$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188216 1072 #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188216 1074 "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{3x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5, 1 \\le y \\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 3, Y \\le 2) &= \\int_{3}^{5}[\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{5}{4x}]_{3}^{5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188217 1069 "e-9191" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188217 1071 "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\textrm{E}[4X-3Y]$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188217 1072 #uuid "136ceff6-b85b-5686-a69b-5d39ff70d3dd" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188217 1074 "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n| $x$$-1$$2$$4$$f_X(x)$$0.35$$0.35$$0.3$ |  | $y$$0$$1$$3$$5$$f_Y(y)$$0.3$$0.2$$0.2$$0.3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}f_{X}(x_{i}) \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot 0.35 + 2 \\cdot 0.35 + 4 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&=-0.35 + 0.7 + 1.2 \\\\\n&= 1.55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)y_{i}f_{Y}(y_{i}) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.3 + 1 \\cdot 0.2 + 3 \\cdot 0.2 + 5 \\cdot 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0 + 0.2 + 0.6 + 1.5 \\\\\n&= 2.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[4X - 3Y] &= 4E[X] - 3E[Y] \\\\\n&= 4(1.55) - 3(2.3) \\\\\n&= 6.2 - 6.9 \\\\\n&=-0.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188218 1069 "e-9223" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188218 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n${1 - \\frac{1}{x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x \\geq 1$ and $y\\geq 1$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188218 1072 #uuid "b48c754c-18c0-575f-bc6a-54f6fbdcec62" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188218 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs $F_X(x)$ and $F_Y(y)$, respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = F_X(x) \\cdot F_Y (y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 1\n$$\n\nthe joint CDF for our random variables is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x, y) &= F_{X}(x) \\cdot F_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{1}{x})(1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}}) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}} + \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\n{1 - \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}} + \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y^{3}}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1, y \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188219 1069 "e-9267" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188219 1071 "In a library, each shelf is filled with $20$ volumes of an encyclopedia, plus one bookend. The weight of one volume, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\textrm g$, while the weight of a bookend, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\textrm g$. If one shelf is randomly selected, what is the probability that it supports a weight of more than $3\\,000\\,\\textrm g$? You may assume that the weights of the volumes and bookend are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 2.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9783 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9821 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9826 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9830 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9834 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9861 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9864 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9868 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9871 \\,$ |" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188219 1072 #uuid "c1eefedf-bc2e-5c6c-84e8-0c80aee4a4cb" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188219 1074 "Let $X_i$ denote the weight of the $i$th volume, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a bookend. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(150, 5^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 20\n$$\n\nand\n$Y \\sim N(50, 2^2)$.\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected shelf. Then, since\n\n$$\nW = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{20} + Y\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[W] &= 20 \\cdot 150 + 50 \\\\\n&= 3050, \\\\\nVar[W] &= 20 \\cdot 5^{2} + 2^{2} \\\\\n&= 504\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW \\sim N(3\\,050, 504)\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W > 3\\,000)$, we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(W > 3000) &= P(Z > \\frac{3000 - 3050}{\\sqrt{504}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z > - 2.23) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 2.23) \\\\\n&= Φ(2.23)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(2.23)\\approx 0.9871\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(W > 3000) \\mid \\approx Φ(2.23); = 0.9871\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188220 1069 "e-9918" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188220 1071 "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188220 1072 #uuid "47507b72-86e2-5980-9e59-22d59c9f7279" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188220 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} &= (12.5)^{2} + (11.7)^{2} + (13.2)^{2} + (12.8)^{2} + (12.1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 156.25 + 136.89 + 174.24 + 163.84 + 146.41 \\\\\n&= 777.63\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188221 1069 "e-9920" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188221 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left(\\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188221 1072 #uuid "11f8e3f1-5110-5db1-83de-99d65ea76de5" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188221 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188222 1069 "e-9987" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188222 1071 "Find $\\text{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$ given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{[x, y, z]^T \\,: \\, x=y=z\\}$." 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188222 1072 #uuid "91885df0-9f1d-5c18-acf1-70259cf7f4d9" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188222 1074 "Any vector from $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ x \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix} = x\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(v_{1})\n$$\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=1\n$$\n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnullity(T) &= 3 - rank(T) \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188906 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188907 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1039 "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188206 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188223 1042 #uuid "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050943 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188848 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188849 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1039 "Rounding Up Using a Number Line" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188182 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188224 1042 #uuid "e827144c-5556-5e9c-9329-6c25eb65e93f" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188593 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188617 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188225 1039 "Rounding Up to the Nearest Ten" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188225 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188183 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188225 1042 #uuid "7a0b6b25-3926-5396-83ca-c012ac1024f0" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188594 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188625 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188226 1039 "Rounding Up to Some Other Place Value" 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188226 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188184 13194139533372 17592186045585 true] [17592186188226 1042 #uuid "cf6dcec4-8f6e-5cd